-- ── 26PCSC01 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Source ONE real list of items you can name (a CSV of your class marks, or the word list from a text file you downloaded). Load N items into a BST you code AND into a plain list, and make your program COUNT the actual comparisons for one search in each.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your terminal showing the comparison counts for 4 growing N values + the named source of your data + a hand-drawn count-vs-N curve. Three sentences: which count matched O(log n), which matched O(n), and where reality diverged from the asymptotic prediction."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Race your OWN Quick Sort against your OWN Merge Sort on the same real dataset (at least 10,000 rows you sourced), timing each with your language's clock on YOUR machine.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the timed runs + a small table (input size, quicksort ms, mergesort ms) from your machine. Three sentences: which won, on which input shape quicksort degraded, and whether the numbers tracked the n·log n curve you expected."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Take ONE real optimisation instance from your own life (your week's tasks with a duration and a value each, OR real coin denominations for a bill you paid). Solve it BOTH with a greedy rule you code and with the true optimum, and compare the two answers.","deliverable_notes":"Your running code for both + the two answers side by side + the actual instance data. Three sentences: did greedy match the optimum, on THIS instance where did it fall short, and what property of the input let greedy win or lose."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Pick ONE DP problem from this unit (0/1 Knapsack, OBST, or All-Pair Shortest Path). Before writing memoised code, hand-fill the DP table on paper for a small instance you invented, then code it and confirm your table matches the machine's.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your hand-filled DP table + a screenshot of your code producing the same table. Three sentences on the moment you SAW the overlapping subproblem, and how spotting that reuse is a skill you can now carry to a new problem."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Implement backtracking for a real instance (N-Queens for growing N, or graph-colouring on a real conflict graph such as your class's exam-clash timetable). Push N up until your machine visibly slows.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot showing solve-time at each N (the point where it explodes) + the real graph/board you used. Three sentences on what it felt like to hit a wall no faster machine fixes, and what that taught you about which problems are practical to solve."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Crossover Point","primary":"Benchmark two algorithms you implement (e.g. insertion vs merge sort, or linear vs binary search) across growing input sizes on a real dataset you sourced, and empirically find the input size where the faster one overtakes the other on YOUR hardware. Bring running code, timing logs and a plotted curve.","support":"~400 words tying the measured curve to asymptotic theory from Units I-II.","llc":"Show the crossover plot and defend, under questions, why the machine disagrees with pure Big-O."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Where Greedy Breaks","primary":"Choose a real optimisation instance you can name (your own timetable, a coin system, a cable-laying map), solve it with a greedy algorithm AND with dynamic programming, and construct a concrete input where greedy provably loses. Bring both programs, the instances and the divergence.","support":"~400 words on the exchange-argument logic of Units III-IV.","llc":"Run both live on a fresh input the cohort hands you and explain who wins and why."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Proof by My Own Hand","primary":"Hand-derive the recurrence for one divide-and-conquer algorithm using the recursion-tree or substitution method on paper, prove the bound, then verify it against runtimes you measured. Bring the hand-written proof (photo) and the measured data that confirms it.","support":"~400 words on how the algebra and the stopwatch agree.","llc":"Reproduce a key step of the proof live on the board and answer the cohort's 'what if' questions."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Shortest Path Home","primary":"Model a real network you can walk (your commute stops, or the campus buildings with measured distances) as a weighted graph, implement Dijkstra or Floyd-Warshall, and validate the computed shortest path against the route you actually take. Bring the graph data, code and a photo of the real route.","support":"~400 words on modelling choices.","llc":"Demo the program finding a path between two nodes the audience picks."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Wall","primary":"Implement backtracking or branch-and-bound for one NP-hard instance (large N-Queens, subset-sum, or a real graph-colouring problem from campus scheduling), instrument it to count nodes explored, and measure exactly where it becomes intractable on your machine. Bring code, the real instance and the explosion data.","support":"~400 words on pruning and why it only delays the wall.","llc":"Run it live and let the cohort push N until it stalls."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSC01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSC02 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Pick ONE real system you use daily (your college library, a canteen order counter, an ATM). Model it as objects: identify the real objects, their state and their behaviour, applying the elements of the object model (abstraction, encapsulation, hierarchy).","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn object diagram of that named real system + a photo/screenshot of it. Three sentences naming three real objects you found, the state each holds, and one abstraction you deliberately chose to hide."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For the SAME real system, map the relationships among its classes: which class 'is-a' another, which 'has-a' another, which 'uses' another. Show at least three distinct relationship types you can justify from the real system.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn class-relationship diagram + three sentences: one real 'is-a' and one real 'has-a' you found in the system, and why misclassifying one would break the design."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Write and compile ONE working C++ program that uses input/output, a control structure, and a function you defined — solving a small real need (e.g. computing your own semester GPA from your real marks).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the compiled program running with YOUR real input + the source. Three sentences: what real numbers you fed it, one bug the compiler caught, and how you fixed it."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Write a small class with a constructor AND a destructor that manages a real resource (opens a file, or allocates an array), then deliberately run it and watch the destructor fire. Prove the resource is released.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot showing constructor and destructor messages printing in order for your real run + the code. Three sentences on why leaving cleanup out matters in real long-running software, and what a leak would cost a user."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Write ONE template (a function or class template) and instantiate it with two different real types (int and string, say). Then write a virtual function and call it through a base pointer to show runtime polymorphism.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the template working for both types + the polymorphic call resolving correctly. Three sentences on how templates let you write once for many types, and how you figured out a compiler error you had not seen before."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A System I Use, Modelled in Objects","primary":"Choose one real system you interact with weekly (library issue-desk, hostel mess billing, bus pass counter), design its class model, and BUILD a working C++ program that implements the core workflow with classes, inheritance and at least one virtual function. Bring compiled code and a demo run on real data.","support":"~400 words on the object-model choices from Units I-II.","llc":"Run the program live and defend why you split responsibilities the way you did."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Overload That Reads Like English","primary":"Pick a real domain type (a Money/Matrix/Time class), implement it in C++ with operator overloading and type conversion so expressions read naturally, and prove correctness against hand-computed results. Bring code, test cases and your hand-worked answers.","support":"~400 words on operator overloading design from Unit IV.","llc":"Type a fresh expression the cohort dictates and show your class evaluate it correctly."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"One Interface, Many Shapes","primary":"Build a small C++ class hierarchy (shapes, accounts, employees) exercising inheritance and runtime polymorphism through a base pointer, and demonstrate that adding a NEW derived class needs zero change to existing calling code. Bring the code and a live before/after.","support":"~400 words on polymorphism from Unit V.","llc":"Add a new subclass live at the podium and show the old code still works unchanged."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Records That Outlive the Program","primary":"Build a C++ program that persists real records to a file (a small inventory, attendance, or contact list you actually maintain), reads them back, and survives being closed and reopened. Bring the code, the data file and a demo of persistence.","support":"~400 words on file handling and exception safety from Unit V.","llc":"Add a record live, close the program, reopen it, and prove the record is still there."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"My Own Generic Container","primary":"Implement a templated container class (a stack, list or pair) in C++ that works for at least three unrelated types you instantiate, with correct construction and destruction proven by trace output. Bring code and the multi-type demo.","support":"~400 words on templates and memory management from Units IV-V.","llc":"Instantiate your container with a type the cohort names on the spot and show it compile and run."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSC02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSC03 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take a real bit of your own data (your month's expenses, or your class's marks) and store it four ways in Python: a list, a tuple, a dict and a set. Do one operation on each (append, index, key-lookup, dedupe) in a live interpreter.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your Python REPL showing all four structures holding your real data + one operation on each. Three sentences: which structure fit which need, and one thing a set did that a list could not."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Write ONE Python program with a loop, a comprehension, a function AND a try/except that solves a small real task (parse your real bank SMS lines, or tally words in a text file you chose).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of it running on YOUR real input + the source. Three sentences: what real input you gave it, which line raised an exception during testing, and how your except block handled it gracefully."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Refactor a working script of yours into a reusable module: move functions into a separate .py file, import it into a second program, and add one class with inheritance. Confirm both programs share the module.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot showing two programs importing your one module + the file layout. Three sentences on what made your code reusable, and how you now decide when to split code into a module versus keep it inline."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Build a tiny end-to-end flow: read structured data from a real file (CSV/JSON you have), write it into an SQLite database with Python, and fetch one record back. Then pull one live value from a real web API (any public JSON endpoint).","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the DB round-trip AND the live API value + the source URL. Three sentences on how file, database and web layers connected in one program, and where the data changed shape between them."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Write a Python program that touches the real system: list files in a named directory, OR spin up two threads/processes and watch them interleave, OR open a socket between two terminals and pass one message.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the real system output (directory listing, interleaved thread prints, or the message crossing the socket). Three sentences on what surprised you about how the OS actually behaved versus how you imagined it."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Script That Saves Me an Hour","primary":"Identify one real repetitive task in your own week (renaming photos, tallying expenses, splitting a PDF, cleaning a messy CSV), build a Python tool that automates it end-to-end, and run it on your real files. Bring the code, before/after files and a timing of hand vs script.","support":"~400 words on the data structures and error handling from Units I-II.","llc":"Run the tool live on a messy input the cohort provides."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Data In, Answer Out","primary":"Source a real dataset you care about (your fitness log, a Kaggle CSV, campus data with permission), build a Python program that loads it into SQLite, runs three real queries, and reports findings. Bring code, the database file and the query results.","support":"~400 words on storage choices from Unit IV.","llc":"Answer a fresh question the audience asks by writing the query live against your database."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"My Own Package","primary":"Design and build a small reusable Python package (multiple modules, classes with inheritance, a clean import surface) that solves a real domain problem you chose, with your own docstrings and a demo script. Bring the package tree and a program that imports it.","support":"~400 words on modules, packages and OOP from Unit III.","llc":"Import your package live and use it to solve a problem you have not shown before."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Talking to the Live Web","primary":"Build a Python program that consumes a real public web API (weather, currency, transit), handles failures and rate limits, and turns live responses into something useful you demonstrate. Bring code, the API source, and captured live runs.","support":"~400 words on web clients and automation from Unit IV.","llc":"Run it live so the cohort sees real, changing data flow through your program."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Two Things at Once","primary":"Build a concurrent Python program that does real work in parallel (fetch several URLs, process files in threads, or a publish-subscribe messenger between terminals), and MEASURE the speed-up versus the sequential version on your machine. Bring both versions and timing evidence.","support":"~400 words on concurrency and networks from Unit V.","llc":"Run sequential then concurrent live and show the measured difference."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSC03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSC04 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take a real dataset you sourced (UCI, Kaggle, or your own collected records) and run a genuine data-mining task on it by hand-first: profile it — count rows, list attributes, note missing values, and state which mining task (classification, clustering, association) it suits.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your profiling output + the dataset's named source. Three sentences: how many real rows and attributes, one data-quality issue you found, and which mining task the data actually supports and why."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Train ONE classifier (decision tree or naive Bayes) on a real labelled dataset you split into train/test, and MEASURE its accuracy on the held-out test set.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your confusion matrix / accuracy on YOUR test split + the dataset source. Three sentences: the real accuracy number, one class it confused, and whether that error would matter in the real decision the data is about."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"On a real dataset, run BOTH a clustering algorithm (k-means) AND association-rule mining (Apriori) — or clustering plus a distance analysis — and see whether the two methods tell a consistent story about the same data.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the clusters AND the top rules/associations + the data source. Three sentences on where the two techniques agreed, where they diverged, and what integrating both revealed that one alone hid."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Design a star schema for a real reporting need you can name (a shop's sales, your college's admissions). Identify the fact table, its measures, and at least three dimension tables. Sketch how one OLAP query (roll-up or drill-down) would run.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn star-schema diagram for that named real domain + three sentences on how you decided what was a fact versus a dimension, and what a roll-up would compute."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Investigate ONE real, named use of data warehousing/mining in government or a public service (a national data warehouse, a ration/health/transport system). Note what data it holds and one benefit AND one risk to real people.","deliverable_notes":"A short profile of that named real system (with a source) + three sentences: one concrete good it does for citizens, one privacy or fairness risk you see, and where you would draw the line."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"What My Data Actually Says","primary":"Source a real dataset you can name and defend, build a full mining pipeline (clean, train a classifier, evaluate on a held-out set), and report an honest accuracy with its errors. Bring code, the dataset, and the confusion matrix.","support":"~400 words on the classification methods from Units I-II.","llc":"Run your model live on a fresh row the cohort supplies and explain its prediction and its confidence."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Hidden Groups","primary":"Apply clustering to a real dataset (customers, students, sensor readings you sourced), determine a defensible number of clusters, and characterise each group with real evidence. Bring the code, the data, and the cluster profiles.","support":"~400 words on clustering and distance measures from Unit III.","llc":"Show the clusters and defend, under questioning, why your k is right and what each group means in the real world."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Rules Nobody Told Me","primary":"Run Apriori (or FP-growth) on a real transaction dataset you sourced (basket data, course enrolments, your own logs), surface association rules with real support and confidence, and validate one rule against reality. Bring code, data and the ranked rules.","support":"~400 words on association-rule quality from Unit III.","llc":"Present your strongest rule and let the cohort challenge whether it is signal or coincidence."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Warehouse for a Real Question","primary":"Design and build a small dimensional warehouse (star schema in SQLite/any DB) for a real reporting need you can name, load real data, and run three OLAP-style queries (roll-up, drill-down, slice). Bring the schema, the loaded data and the query outputs.","support":"~400 words on warehousing and OLAP from Unit IV.","llc":"Answer a fresh analytical question live by writing the query against your warehouse."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Mining With a Conscience","primary":"Take a real dataset about people, run a genuine mining task, then AUDIT your own result for bias or privacy leakage — measure whether it treats subgroups unequally, and propose a concrete mitigation you test. Bring the model, the audit numbers and the fix.","support":"~400 words on the social implications from Units I and V.","llc":"Present the harm you found and defend your mitigation against the cohort's objections."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSC04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSC05 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Write a Java program that spawns two threads doing real work (one counting, one printing) and observe their interleaving; add one event-handled Swing/AWT component (a button that reacts to your click).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the interleaved thread output AND the button reacting + the code. Three sentences: what order the threads actually ran in on your machine, and why the interleaving changed between runs."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Build a minimal RMI application: a remote object with one method, a server that registers it, and a client that calls it from a second terminal (or second process). Make the method return real data.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the server running and the client receiving the remote return value across the two terminals + the interface code. Three sentences: what the client asked, what came back, and one RMI setup error you had to solve."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Write a JDBC program that connects to a real database, CREATEs a table, INSERTs at least three real rows you typed, and SELECTs them back into your Java program.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the round-trip (insert then select printing your real rows) + the code. Three sentences on how Java and SQL passed data between them, and one place a type mismatch or SQL error bit you."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Build a working Servlet or JSP page that reads real data from an HTML form a person fills (a feedback form, a marks-entry form) and responds with a computed result. Submit it yourself from a browser.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the browser form AND the server's response for YOUR real submission + the code. Three sentences on what a real user would experience, and one thing you changed to make the page clearer for them."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Extend one of your earlier programs with an advanced technique you had not used (session tracking with cookies, a JSP session object, or a servlet reading HTTP headers), figuring out the API from the docs yourself.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the new capability working (e.g. a session persisting across two requests) + the code. Three sentences on how you learned the unfamiliar API, and what you would search for faster next time."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Web App People Actually Fill","primary":"Build a working Servlet+JSP web application for a real need you can name (feedback collection, marks entry, a purchase order), backed by a JDBC database, that a person uses from a browser end-to-end. Bring the running app and real submissions.","support":"~400 words on the servlet/JSP request lifecycle from Unit IV.","llc":"Have a cohort member fill your form live and show the data land in your database."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Two Machines, One Call","primary":"Build a genuine distributed Java application over RMI (or sockets) where a client on one process invokes real work on a server process and gets live results back. Bring the code and a demo across two terminals/machines.","support":"~400 words on remote invocation from Unit II.","llc":"Run client and server live in front of the cohort and let someone trigger a remote call."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Payslip That Computes Itself","primary":"Build a JSP application that takes real inputs (hours, marks, order lines) and generates a correct computed document (payslip, mark sheet, invoice), verified against your hand-calculated answer. Bring the running page and your manual check.","support":"~400 words on JSP scriptlets and expressions from Unit IV.","llc":"Enter fresh numbers the cohort dictates and show the document compute correctly live."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Data That Survives the Session","primary":"Build a Java web app using JDBC plus session tracking so a user's state persists across pages (a cart, a multi-step form, a login), and prove the data is stored and retrieved correctly. Bring code and a demo of state surviving navigation.","support":"~400 words on sessions and JDBC from Units III-V.","llc":"Walk the cohort through a multi-page flow live and show their data persist."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Threads Doing Real Work","primary":"Build a multithreaded Java program that solves a real concurrent problem (parallel file processing, a producer-consumer queue, a small chat over sockets) and MEASURE its behaviour under load on your machine. Bring code and timing/interleaving evidence.","support":"~400 words on threading and networking from Unit I.","llc":"Run it live and let the cohort observe threads interleaving in real time."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSC05'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSCP01 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-3","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"For the Tower of Hanoi, BST-traversal and stack-using-linked-list programs, HAND-TRACE the recursion/pointer moves for a small input on paper BEFORE running, then run your code and diff your trace against the machine's output.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your hand-trace (Hanoi moves for 3 disks, or the BST in-order sequence) beside a screenshot of your program's actual output. Three sentences: where your paper trace and the machine agreed, and the one step you had wrong until the code corrected you."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 4-6","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Run your Circular Queue, Quick Sort and Heap Sort programs on real inputs YOU chose (feed quicksort an already-sorted array to trigger its worst case), and instrument each to count operations or print each pass.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots showing each program's real output + the worst-case input you fed quicksort. Three sentences: how many passes heapsort took, what happened to quicksort on the sorted input, and why."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 7-9","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Across the Knapsack, Divide-and-Conquer and Two-Queens programs, identify which algorithmic design strategy each one embodies, and for the knapsack solve the SAME small instance by both your greedy intuition and the program's method.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of each program running + a note pairing each with its design paradigm. Three sentences on where your greedy hand-answer and the program's answer diverged on the knapsack, and what that says about the two strategies."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 10-12","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"For the Virtual Function, Parameterized Constructor and Friend Function programs, deliberately BREAK each one (remove the 'virtual' keyword, drop a constructor argument, remove the friend declaration) and observe the exact compiler/behaviour change.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the working version AND the broken version's error/wrong output for each + the code change you made. Three sentences on what the virtual keyword actually changed at runtime, proven by your before/after."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 13-15","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Extend the Employee-Details-using-Files program into something a real office could use: store at least five real (or realistic) employee records, then close and reopen the program to prove the data persisted on disk.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the records surviving a program restart + the data file on disk. Three sentences on what a real HR clerk would need that your program still lacks, and one improvement you would prioritise for them."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Worst Case I Provoked","primary":"Take one sorting or search algorithm from the lab, construct the input that forces its worst case, instrument the code to count operations, and MEASURE the gap between best, average and worst on your machine. Bring the code, the crafted inputs and the operation counts.","support":"~400 words on complexity from the lab.","llc":"Feed your algorithm a fresh input the cohort supplies and predict, then show, how many operations it takes."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Every Design Method, One Problem","primary":"Pick one real problem and solve it three ways using lab techniques (divide-and-conquer, greedy, and backtracking/exhaustive), then compare correctness and cost of all three on the same inputs. Bring the three implementations and the comparison data.","support":"~400 words on the design paradigms from the lab.","llc":"Run all three live on an input the cohort chooses and defend which method you would ship."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"An OOP System That Does One Real Job","primary":"Build a C++ program using inheritance, virtual functions, constructors and file storage that manages one real record-keeping task (employees, library, inventory) end-to-end with persistence. Bring the code, the data file and a demo.","support":"~400 words on the OOP features from the lab.","llc":"Add a record live, restart the program, and show the data persisted, answering the cohort's questions about your class design."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Proven Correct by Hand","primary":"Choose one lab algorithm, hand-trace its full execution on a non-trivial input on paper, then run instrumented code that prints each step, and prove the machine matches your trace exactly. Bring the hand-trace photo and the step-by-step program output.","support":"~400 words on how the algorithm works internally.","llc":"Reproduce a key step of the trace live on the board and answer 'what happens if the input changes' questions."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"My Own Data Structure Library","primary":"Package your stack, queue, BST and heap implementations into one reusable, tested C++ library with a clean interface, and demonstrate a small application that uses all of them together. Bring the library, tests and the demo app.","support":"~400 words on when each structure is the right choice.","llc":"Use your library live to solve a small problem the cohort poses, choosing structures on the spot."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSCP01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSCP02 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Using the elementary-data-items and conditional-branch programs, load a real slice of your own data (your week's expenses or marks) into lists, dicts and tuples, and write conditionals that classify the entries (e.g. flag every expense over a threshold).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the program classifying YOUR real data + the data itself. Three sentences: what real threshold you set, how many entries each branch caught, and one case that surprised you."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-4","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"In the loops and functions programs, refactor a repetitive block into a function and drive it with a loop over real inputs; compare the line count and readability before and after.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the refactored program running on real inputs + the before/after line counts. Three sentences on what the function let you stop repeating, and one bug the refactor exposed."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 5-6","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"For the exception-handling and inheritance programs, deliberately feed inputs that crash the naive version (bad type, missing key, divide-by-zero), then wrap them in try/except; separately, add a subclass that overrides a parent method.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the program surviving a crash-input it previously died on + the override working. Three sentences on how you discovered which exception type to catch, and how you would debug an unfamiliar error faster next time."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 7-8","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Combine the polymorphism and file-operations programs: write objects of different subclasses to a file and read them back, so persistence and polymorphism work together on real data you enter.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of mixed objects being saved and correctly reloaded from disk + the file. Three sentences on how polymorphism and file I/O connected in one flow, and where the data changed form between object and file."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 9-10","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Build the dynamic-web-page-with-forms program into a small page a real person could fill (a feedback or sign-up form), organised using your own module, and submit it yourself from a browser.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the browser form AND the server handling YOUR real submission + the module file. Three sentences on what a first-time user would find confusing, and one change you made so the form felt clearer to them."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Tool I Built for Myself","primary":"Identify one real repetitive task in your own life, build a Python tool (functions, loops, exception handling, file I/O) that automates it, and run it on your real files with a hand vs tool timing. Bring code, real before/after files and the timing.","support":"~400 words on the Python constructs used, from the lab.","llc":"Run the tool live on messy input the cohort provides and show it survive bad data."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Crash-Proof","primary":"Take a working program and harden it — enumerate the real ways users could break it, add targeted exception handling for each, and PROVE with a test log that it now survives every bad input you throw. Bring the code and the crash-then-survive evidence.","support":"~400 words on error handling from the lab.","llc":"Invite the cohort to try to crash your program live and show it hold."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"An Object Model for a Real Thing","primary":"Model a real domain (a library, a playlist, a gradebook) with a Python class hierarchy using inheritance and polymorphism, persist it to a file, and demonstrate the full lifecycle on real data you enter. Bring the code, the data file and a demo.","support":"~400 words on OOP from the lab.","llc":"Add a new subclass live and show existing code handle it without changes."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Page Someone Fills","primary":"Build a working dynamic web page with a form that collects, validates and stores real user input, organised into your own modules, and have a real person use it end-to-end. Bring the running page and captured real submissions.","support":"~400 words on web forms and modules from the lab.","llc":"Have a cohort member fill the form live and show the data captured and validated."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"My Reusable Toolkit","primary":"Consolidate your lab work into a reusable Python module/package with clean functions, classes and docstrings, and write a driver program that imports it to solve a real problem you chose. Bring the package tree and the driver.","support":"~400 words on modules and reuse from the lab.","llc":"Import your toolkit live and use it to solve a fresh small problem the cohort poses."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSCP02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSCP03 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Run the Apriori program in R on a real transaction dataset you sourced (a basket dataset, your own shopping/enrolment records, or a Kaggle transactions file), tuning support and confidence until you get non-trivial rules.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the top association rules with their real support/confidence + the dataset source. Three sentences: your strongest rule in plain words, the support and confidence thresholds you chose, and whether the rule is real signal or coincidence."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 2-3","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"On the SAME real dataset, run BOTH k-means and one hierarchical clustering in R, and compare whether the two methods group the points the same way.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the k-means clusters AND the dendrogram + the dataset source. Three sentences on where the two clusterings agreed, where they disagreed, and how you chose k for k-means."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 4-5","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Train a classifier AND a decision tree in R on a real labelled dataset you split into train/test, and MEASURE the accuracy of each on the held-out test set.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of both models' accuracy/confusion matrices on YOUR test split + the plotted decision tree. Three sentences: the two accuracy numbers, which model you trust more for this data, and one feature the tree split on first and why that makes sense."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 6","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Fit a linear regression in R on a real dataset with a genuine relationship you suspect (study hours vs marks, ad spend vs sales), then CHECK the assumptions (look at residuals, R-squared) rather than trusting the line blindly.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your fitted line, its R-squared and the residual plot + the data source. Three sentences on what R-squared told you, one assumption that looked violated, and how you learned to read the residual plot."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 7","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Turn one finding from your earlier labs into a clear R visualization (ggplot) aimed at a non-technical person, then show it to one real person and note whether they read it correctly.","deliverable_notes":"The chart image + a one-line note of what the real viewer understood (or misread). Three sentences on the design choice you made for clarity, what the viewer misread, and the one change you made in response."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Rules Hidden in My Data","primary":"Run Apriori in R on a real transaction dataset you sourced, surface association rules with genuine support and confidence, and validate your top rule against reality or a domain expert. Bring the R code, the dataset and the ranked rules.","support":"~400 words on association-rule mining from the lab.","llc":"Present your strongest rule and let the cohort challenge whether it is causal, correlational or coincidental."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Groups the Data Chose","primary":"Cluster a real dataset in R with both k-means and hierarchical methods, justify your number of clusters with a real criterion (elbow/silhouette), and characterise each group with evidence. Bring the code, the data, and the cluster profiles.","support":"~400 words on clustering from the lab.","llc":"Show the clusters and defend, under questioning, why your k is right and what each cluster means."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"My Model, My Errors","primary":"Build a classifier or decision tree in R on a real labelled dataset, report honest test-set accuracy, and DISSECT its mistakes — which classes it confuses and what that would cost in the real decision. Bring the model, the confusion matrix and the error analysis.","support":"~400 words on classification evaluation from the lab.","llc":"Run the model live on a fresh case the cohort supplies and explain its prediction and where it might be wrong."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Does the Line Hold?","primary":"Fit a regression in R on a real dataset, then STRESS-TEST it — check residuals, try predictions on new points, and honestly report where the model breaks down. Bring the code, the data, the diagnostics and a failed prediction.","support":"~400 words on regression assumptions from the lab.","llc":"Predict a value the cohort asks for live and defend how much to trust it."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"One Chart, No Words Needed","primary":"Take a real finding from your own mining work and craft an R visualization so clear a non-technical person reads it correctly, tested on at least two real viewers with their feedback captured. Bring the chart, the underlying data and the viewer notes.","support":"~400 words on the visualization choices from the lab.","llc":"Show the chart cold to the cohort and see if they read your finding without you narrating it."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSCP03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSCP04 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Get the welcome Servlet and the Purchase-Order-form Servlet running in a real container (Tomcat), and view each in a browser; capture the actual HTTP request/response for the welcome page from the browser's network tab.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the servlet output in the browser AND the captured request/response headers + the code. Three sentences on what the container did between your click and the response, and one deployment error you had to solve."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-4","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Run the form-data and headers-table servlets: submit a real form with your own values and, in the headers servlet, inspect the actual HTTP headers your browser sent.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of your submitted form data echoed back AND the real header table your browser produced + the code. Three sentences on one header you did not expect your browser to send, and what a servlet could do with it."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 5-7","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Across the percentage, purchase-order and payslip JSP programs, notice the repeated pattern (form -> compute -> formatted output) and refactor the payslip to reuse a shared computation, verifying the payslip against YOUR hand-calculation.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the payslip JSP output beside your hand-calculated figures + the code. Three sentences on the pattern you spotted across the three JSPs, and how recognising it made the third one faster to build."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 8-9","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Combine the session-object JSP and the JDBC CRUD program: let a user log an item across pages using a session, and persist it to a real database table with JDBC. Prove state survives navigation AND lands in the DB.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the value persisting across two pages AND appearing in the database table + the code. Three sentences on how the session and the database played different roles, and which data belonged in which."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 10-12","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Get one networked program running across two processes/terminals — the RMI client-server, OR the socket text-messaging program — and pass a real message you type between them.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of your typed message crossing from one terminal to the other + the code. Three sentences on what it felt like to see two separate programs actually talk, and one real application this pattern could power."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Web App on a Real Server","primary":"Build and deploy a working Servlet+JSP application on Tomcat for a real need you can name (feedback, ordering, marks entry), backed by a JDBC database, used end-to-end from a browser. Bring the deployed app and real submissions in the database.","support":"~400 words on the servlet/JSP lifecycle from the lab.","llc":"Have a cohort member use your app live and show their data land in the database."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Document That Computes Itself","primary":"Build a JSP application that generates a correct computed document (payslip, mark sheet, invoice) from real inputs, verified against your own hand-calculation for several cases. Bring the running page and your manual checks.","support":"~400 words on JSP computation from the lab.","llc":"Enter fresh figures the cohort dictates and show the document compute the right result live."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"State That Remembers the User","primary":"Build a multi-page Java web flow (a cart, a multi-step form, a login) using session tracking plus JDBC persistence, and prove a user's data survives navigation AND a restart. Bring the code and a demo of state persisting.","support":"~400 words on sessions and JDBC from the lab.","llc":"Walk the cohort through the multi-page flow live and show their data persist across pages and storage."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Two Programs Talking","primary":"Build a real networked Java application (RMI service or socket chat) where two processes exchange live data across terminals or machines, handling at least one failure case gracefully. Bring the code and a two-terminal demo.","support":"~400 words on RMI/sockets from the lab.","llc":"Run both ends live and let a cohort member send a message or trigger a remote call across the connection."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"From Form to Database, Fully Wired","primary":"Build a complete Java web feature that takes a browser form, validates it in a servlet/JSP, stores it via JDBC, and reads it back into a listing page — every layer working on real data. Bring the running feature and the populated database.","support":"~400 words integrating servlets, JSP and JDBC from the lab.","llc":"Add a record through the browser live and show it appear in the listing, backed by the database."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSCP04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSE01 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"On your own Linux machine (or a lab machine you name), run `ps -eLf`, `vmstat 1 5` and `cat /proc/loadavg` while you start one CPU-heavy process you write yourself. Capture how the scheduler and load average respond.","deliverable_notes":"Your terminal screenshots (with hostname visible) + the small program you ran + 3 sentences on what the numbers showed about process scheduling. Not a description of scheduling from a book — your machine's actual output."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Implement Lamport's logical clock for a 3-process message exchange (write the code yourself). Feed it a specific message ordering you choose and show the timestamps it produces.","deliverable_notes":"Your running code + the output timestamp table for your chosen message sequence + 2 lines on one ordering it got right that wall-clock time would get wrong."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Take one real-time task set you define (3–4 tasks with periods and execution times) and work the Rate-Monotonic schedulability test by hand; then verify by simulating the first hyperperiod.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-worked utilisation bound + the hand-drawn schedule timeline for one hyperperiod + one line stating whether your set is schedulable and why."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Take the handheld device you actually own; identify which OS layer (Android architecture layer) three of its permission prompts belong to, by reading its real settings screens.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the three real permission screens on your device + your mapping of each to an Android architecture layer + 2 lines on one security trade-off you can now see."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"On a Linux system, trace one file read end-to-end using `strace` on a small program you write; identify the system calls that cross into the I/O and file subsystems you studied.","deliverable_notes":"Your annotated `strace` excerpt (real output) + your program + 3 sentences naming which calls touched the memory-management vs file-system paths."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Scheduler I Measured, Not Read About","primary":"On a machine you name, design a reproducible experiment that makes the Linux scheduler visibly favour one workload over another (e.g. nice values, cgroup limits) and capture the measurements that prove it happened.","support":"~400 words on what the numbers taught you that the textbook description did not.","llc":"Run or replay the experiment live and answer 'what if I changed this parameter?' unscripted."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Logical Time, Built and Broken","primary":"Implement Lamport clocks AND a vector-clock variant for the same message trace, and construct one concrete scenario where Lamport clocks cannot detect a causality that vector clocks can.","support":"~400 words on why the extra state buys that power.","llc":"Demonstrate both on your trace and let an examiner propose a new message ordering to test live."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Is My Task Set Schedulable? I Proved It","primary":"Define a real-time task set (justify the numbers from a plausible embedded scenario you describe) and produce a complete hand analysis under both Rate-Monotonic and Earliest-Deadline-First, plus a simulated timeline that confirms it.","support":"~400 words on the gap between the utilisation bound and the actual behaviour.","llc":"Defend each step; faculty may add one task and ask you to re-reason."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Permission Path on My Own Phone","primary":"Take your own handheld device, choose three sensitive capabilities (camera, location, contacts) and trace, using the device's real screens and public Android documentation, how a request travels through the OS layers to the hardware.","support":"~400 words on one security weakness you can now argue for.","llc":"Walk the cohort through the path on your actual device."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A System-Call Story I Can Defend","primary":"Pick ONE operation (open-read-close, or a fork+exec) and produce a fully traced, annotated account of every system call it makes on a real Linux box, mapping each to the kernel subsystem it enters.","support":"~400 words on the one call whose purpose surprised you.","llc":"Reconstruct the trace live and answer 'why does this call happen here?'"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSE01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSE06 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Using a field-strength or network app on your own phone (e.g. the phone's field-test/engineering screen), record signal strength (dBm) at five named locations on campus or your route.","deliverable_notes":"Your five readings with the exact place names + a hand-drawn simple map + 2 lines linking one weak spot to a physical cause you studied."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Identify the actual cell your phone connects to at home (cell ID / tower via a public tower-map or the phone's engineering mode) and at college; compute the approximate distance to each.","deliverable_notes":"The two cell IDs you found + the tower coordinates + your distance computation shown by hand + 2 lines on frequency reuse you can now see locally."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For one satellite-based service you use (GPS/GNSS), log the number of satellites your phone locks onto (a GNSS-status app) indoors vs outdoors at a named place.","deliverable_notes":"Your two satellite-count readings (screenshots) at the named place + 3 sentences relating the difference to the propagation issues in Unit III."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Capture your phone's IP as it moves between two Wi-Fi networks (or Wi-Fi to mobile data) and observe what changes; relate it to Mobile IP's care-of-address idea.","deliverable_notes":"The before/after IP details you captured + 3 sentences on which Mobile-IP mechanism keeps a session alive across the change."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Interview one person in your family or neighbourhood (name them, with consent) who has used mobile phones since 2G; record how the generations changed one specific thing they do.","deliverable_notes":"3–4 quoted sentences from the named person + your one-line mapping of their story to a 2G→4G technical change."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Signal Map of a Place I Know","primary":"Measure and map real signal strength across a named area (10+ points, your own readings) and explain the strong/weak pattern using cellular propagation and reuse concepts.","support":"~400 words on what the physical geography did to the radio.","llc":"Present your map and defend one predicted weak spot the audience can test."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Which Tower Am I On? A Local Investigation","primary":"Determine, for several named locations, which cell your device attaches to and reason about handover boundaries you can actually observe by walking a route.","support":"~400 words on the mobility-management decisions the network made for you.","llc":"Walk the cohort through your route data and answer 'why did it hand over here?'"},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Handover I Watched Happen","primary":"Design a repeatable observation (a walk or drive route you name) that forces at least one visible network change — cell handover, Wi-Fi-to-cellular, or IP change — and capture the evidence with timestamps.","support":"~400 words connecting your trace to the standards in Units II–IV.","llc":"Replay the trace and reason live about an alternative route."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Four Generations, One Real Voice","primary":"Conduct named, consented interviews with two or three people of different ages about how mobile technology changed one concrete activity (banking, farming prices, staying in touch) and ground each change in a specific technical generation.","support":"~400 words on the human dimension the specs leave out.","llc":"Present the voices and defend your technical mapping."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"GNSS: Where the Sky Matters","primary":"Run a structured set of your own GNSS-lock measurements across contrasting named environments (open field, indoors, under trees) and explain the results with satellite-visibility and propagation theory.","support":"~400 words on the gap between the ideal model and your data.","llc":"Show the data and answer a 'what about this location?' question live."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSE06'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSE08 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take a real snippet of source code you wrote (5-10 lines in any language) and hand-trace it through the compiler phases: mark the tokens the lexer would emit, then the parse structure. Build a small lexer (regex-based) that tokenises the same snippet.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your hand-marked token list on the real snippet + a screenshot of your lexer tokenising it. Three sentences: how many tokens your code produced, one token your lexer initially mis-split, and how you fixed the rule."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Write a context-free grammar for a small real language you define (arithmetic expressions, a config format), and build a parser (recursive-descent or using a tool) that accepts valid strings and rejects invalid ones you throw at it.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your parser accepting three valid and rejecting three invalid inputs YOU wrote + the grammar. Three sentences on one ambiguity you had to remove from your grammar, and how you knew it was ambiguous."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For one real expression (e.g. a = b*c + d), hand-produce three-address intermediate code, then sketch the target code a simple code generator would emit for it. Connect the front-end parse to the back-end output.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your hand-written three-address code AND target code for that real expression. Three sentences on how the intermediate form decoupled the language from the machine, and one place your target code was clearly improvable."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Take a basic block of code (yours) and OPTIMISE it by hand: apply constant folding, common-subexpression elimination and dead-code removal. Count operations before and after.","deliverable_notes":"A photo showing the before and after code with your optimisations marked + the op counts. Three sentences on which optimisation saved the most, and how you now recognise an optimisation opportunity you would have missed before."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Investigate how a real program you wrote actually uses memory at runtime: draw the activation record for one function call (parameters, locals, return address) and reason about how recursion would stack these frames.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn stack diagram for two nested/recursive calls of YOUR real function + three sentences on what the runtime does that your source code hid from you, and why a deep recursion could overflow the stack."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"My Own Little Language","primary":"Design a tiny language (calculator, config, or mini-scripting) and BUILD a working front-end — a lexer and parser you implement — that accepts valid programs and reports real syntax errors with positions. Bring the code and a demo on inputs you wrote.","support":"~400 words on lexical and syntax analysis from Units I-II.","llc":"Type a fresh program the cohort dictates and show your parser accept or reject it and locate the error."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"From Expression to Machine","primary":"Build a translator that takes real arithmetic/boolean expressions and emits three-address intermediate code, then a simple target sequence, verified to compute the right value. Bring the code and traced outputs for expressions you chose.","support":"~400 words on intermediate and target code generation from Unit III.","llc":"Run your translator live on an expression the audience supplies and walk through the generated code."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Optimiser's Eye","primary":"Build a small optimiser pass (constant folding, common-subexpression elimination, or dead-code removal) over intermediate code or a real basic block, and MEASURE the reduction in operations on real inputs. Bring code and before/after op counts.","support":"~400 words on optimisation principles from Unit IV.","llc":"Show a live before/after on a block the cohort hands you and defend that your transform preserves meaning."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Where NFA Meets DFA","primary":"Implement the regular-expression-to-NFA-to-DFA pipeline (Thompson construction + subset construction + minimisation) for real regexes you supply, and prove your DFA accepts exactly the right strings. Bring the code and the state diagrams it produces.","support":"~400 words on token recognition from Unit I.","llc":"Feed your DFA a fresh string the cohort names and show it accept or reject, tracing the states."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Inside the Running Program","primary":"Instrument a real recursive program of yours to reveal its runtime behaviour — capture the actual call stack depth, activation records, or parameter-passing at each level — and reconcile it with the theory of storage allocation. Bring the code and the captured runtime evidence.","support":"~400 words on runtime environments from Unit V.","llc":"Run it live, push recursion depth up, and show the cohort the stack grow toward its limit."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSE08'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSE10 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Find ONE real large dataset you can name (an open-data portal file, your phone's exported location/health history, a public log) and characterise it against the 3 Vs: measure its actual Volume (size on disk), describe its Velocity (how fast it grows) and its Variety (structured/semi/unstructured).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the file's real size + the named source. Three sentences giving concrete numbers for Volume and Variety, and one honest judgement on whether this is truly 'big data' or just a large file, and why."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Install/run Hadoop (local or pseudo-distributed) OR use HDFS in a sandbox, put a real file into HDFS, and list/read it back through HDFS commands.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your HDFS commands storing and listing YOUR real file + the block/replication info shown. Three sentences on what HDFS did with your file that a normal filesystem does not, and one setup hurdle you cleared."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Write and run ONE MapReduce job (word count, or a real aggregation over a dataset you sourced), identifying your own mapper, reducer and combiner logic before coding it.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the job completing with YOUR real output + the input source. Three sentences on the mental shift MapReduce required versus a normal loop, and the moment the map-then-reduce decomposition clicked for you."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Load the SAME real dataset into MongoDB (documents) AND model it for Cassandra (column-family/CQL), then run one query in each. Compare how each store shaped the data.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of one query returning your real data from BOTH stores + the dataset source. Three sentences on how the document model and the column model forced different schemas for the same data, and which fit your access pattern better."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Take a real analytical question someone would actually ask of your dataset, answer it with a Hive HQL or Pig Latin script, and turn the result into ONE clear report/chart a non-technical person could read.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the HQL/Pig script AND the resulting report/chart + the question it answers. Three sentences on the real decision your report supports, and one thing you simplified so a non-technical reader would trust it."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Is It Really Big?","primary":"Source a real dataset, measure it honestly against Volume/Velocity/Variety, then process it two ways — a single-machine script AND a distributed approach (HDFS/MapReduce) — and MEASURE where distribution starts to pay off. Bring both pipelines and the timing evidence.","support":"~400 words on the big-data rationale from Units I-II.","llc":"Show your measured crossover and defend whether this dataset genuinely needed Hadoop."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Word Count and Beyond","primary":"Build a real MapReduce job that answers a genuine aggregation question over a dataset you sourced (not the toy example), run it to completion, and validate the output against a hand-computed spot check. Bring code, the input and the verified output.","support":"~400 words on the mapper/reducer/combiner design from Unit III.","llc":"Run the job live and explain, under questioning, how it would scale to ten times the data."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Same Data, Two Databases","primary":"Model one real dataset in BOTH MongoDB and Cassandra, load real records, run the same three queries against each, and MEASURE/compare how each store performs and constrains you. Bring both schemas, the data and the query results.","support":"~400 words on NoSQL data models from Unit IV.","llc":"Run a query the cohort picks against both stores and defend which you would choose for that access pattern."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Report a Manager Would Read","primary":"Take a real dataset and a real question a decision-maker would ask, answer it with Hive/Pig, and produce a Jasper (or equivalent) report/dashboard that a non-technical person could act on. Bring the scripts, the report and the question.","support":"~400 words on the Hive/Pig/reporting layer from Unit V.","llc":"Present the report to the cohort as if they were the managers and defend its conclusion under their questions."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"My End-to-End Pipeline","primary":"Build a complete big-data pipeline on a dataset you sourced — ingest into HDFS, process with MapReduce or Pig, store results in a NoSQL store, and surface one insight — proving each stage ran on real data. Bring the full pipeline and stage-by-stage evidence.","support":"~400 words integrating Units II-V.","llc":"Run the pipeline live end-to-end and let the cohort probe any stage."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSE10'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PCSED1 · m-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Pick ONE real business you can physically walk into and name it — a specific pharmacy, textile showroom, petrol pump, or clinic in your town. Trace how ONE single transaction (a sale, a stock reorder, a patient registration) travels from where data is entered, to where it is stored, to what output it produces.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn information-flow diagram of that named business (input to process to storage to output to feedback) + a photo of the actual system in use — the billing screen, register, or ledger — with the shop named and a dated slip or your hand in frame. Write 3-4 sentences naming which parts are the information system and what would break if it vanished for one day."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Open the 'About / System' panel of ONE real computer you can access — a lab machine, a shop's billing PC, your own laptop — and record its actual CPU model, RAM size, and storage. Then walk the machine and list every piece of software running on it, split into system software and application software.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of the real System panel showing the specs + a hand-written table splitting that machine's software into System vs Application (at least 6 named items). Write 3-4 sentences: which single spec limits this machine most, and whether a shop billing all day would outgrow it and why."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Take real data from a source you can actually reach — a shop's stock list, a class attendance sheet, your household's monthly expenses (at least 15 records) — and organize it into a proper relational structure in a spreadsheet or a free DBMS (Google Sheets, LibreOffice Base, or MySQL).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of your actual table(s) with the real data visible + a hand-drawn logical data model (entities, fields, one relationship). Write 3-4 sentences on one query or sort you ran, and what the old flat-file version could NOT do that your organized database version now can."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Find ONE real person who runs or works in a business that adopted an online or enterprise system in the last few years — a shopkeeper who joined Flipkart/Amazon, an accountant who moved to Tally/ERP, a clerk who started using a hospital system. Ask them, in their own words, what changed in their daily work.","deliverable_notes":"A phone-verifiable name and contact of the person, two or three direct quotes in their own language (with consent), and the name of the system they use. Write 3-4 sentences: what got easier, what got harder, and one human cost or gain the textbook never mentions."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Audit ONE real digital service you personally use — a bank app, the college portal, an e-commerce site, a UPI app — for how it handles YOUR data. Look at what it asks for, what permissions it demands, what it stores, and whether it tells you honestly.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of one real privacy, permission, or consent screen from that named service + a hand-listed table of 3 data-ethics or security risks you found (weak password rule, over-broad permission, no clear logout, silent data sharing). Write 3-4 sentences on the one risk that would worry you most as a user and the one fix you would demand."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Information System Behind One Real Counter","primary":"Choose and name ONE real business you can revisit. Document its complete information system end-to-end — hardware, software, data storage, people, and data flow — through observation plus a staff interview, with photos of the actual screens and devices and a full hand-built system map and component inventory.","support":"~400 words linking what you saw to Units I-V — the IS type, its data model, the functions it serves, its security posture.","llc":"Show your map and defend, under questions, the single upgrade that would help that business most."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Database I Built From Real Records","primary":"Source real data you gather yourself — a shop's inventory, a club's members, a household ledger (30+ records) — then design and build a working relational database (3+ related tables) in MySQL, LibreOffice Base, or Sheets, with at least three queries that answer real questions. Bring the running database.","support":"~400 words on your data-model choices, one normalization decision, and one query result that surprised you.","llc":"Run a query the cohort picks on the spot and explain the result live."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"From Paper to System: A Development Story","primary":"Find ONE real process still run on paper or a messy sheet — a shop's credit book, a temple donation register, a hostel mess count. Apply the SDLC end-to-end: investigate, analyse, design, and build a working prototype (a form plus database, or a no-code app). Bring before/after evidence.","support":"~400 words walking through your SDLC stages and the ethics and security choices you made for real people's data.","llc":"Demo the prototype live and take unscripted questions on what could fail."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The E-Commerce Checkout I Took Apart","primary":"Pick ONE real transaction system you can use — a live online store, a UPI payment, a fee portal. Place a small refundable order or trace one transaction end-to-end, capturing every screen, every data field requested, and the confirmation trail in your own screenshots, then build a step-by-step transaction-processing map.","support":"~400 words classifying the system (TPS / e-commerce type), its data flow, and its trust and security signals.","llc":"Walk the cohort through your captured transaction and defend where you would or wouldn't trust it."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"One Business, One Owner, One System","primary":"Interview ONE named owner or manager who runs on an information system — a Tally accountant, an ERP manufacturer, an online seller. Record their voice with consent, keep a phone-verifiable contact, and quote them in their own language on how IT changed their business, costs, and risks.","support":"~400 words connecting their lived account to Units I-V.","llc":"Play or read their words and tell the cohort what a real practitioner taught you that the textbook did not."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PCSED1'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAC01 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Get ONE real dataset you can point to and touch — your phone's Screen-Time export, a named shop's daily sales register, a hospital OPD sheet, a season of cricket scorecards. Classify every column by data type and measurement scale.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot/photo of the raw source (with a column you circle) + a table listing each column's type (quantitative/qualitative, discrete/continuous, cross-sectional/time-series) and its scale (nominal/ordinal/interval/ratio). Write 3-4 sentences on the one column that resisted classification and what made it ambiguous."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Build a chart (histogram, bar, or pie) from a real dataset you collected, then show it to ONE named real person who is NOT in your class — a shopkeeper, a parent, a hostel-mate — and ask them what it says.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your chart + the person's name and one sentence of their reaction (quoted). Write 3-4 sentences: what they understood correctly, what they misread, and one design change you'd make so a non-analyst reads it right."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Take a real univariate dataset of at least 30 values you sourced yourself (marks, ages, prices, wait-times) and compute mean, median, SD, skewness, and draw a box-plot BY HAND before touching software.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-drawn box-plot (photo) + your computed five-number summary and skewness + the outlier(s) you marked. Write 3-4 sentences: which value is an outlier, whether it's a genuine extreme or a recording error, and how you decided."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Pick TWO variables you can measure over the same 30+ real units (study-hours vs marks, temperature vs cold-drink sales, sleep vs mood). Draw the scatter and compute the correlation coefficient and rank correlation.","deliverable_notes":"The scatter diagram (hand or software) + both correlation values + your raw paired data. Write 3-4 sentences arguing whether the relationship is causal or coincidental, naming one lurking variable that could explain it."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Fit a regression line to a real dataset you sourced, use it to predict one value, then plot the residuals (actual minus predicted) and look for pattern.","deliverable_notes":"Your regression line + equation + a residual plot (hand or software). Write 3-4 sentences on what the residuals told you that the R-value hid — did an assumption break, and how would you check yourself next time before trusting the line?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Dataset I Built With My Own Hands","primary":"Gather a real dataset of at least 50 observations yourself (survey your hostel wing, log a named shop's sales for a week, record a process you can name). Produce a complete descriptive report from YOUR raw data: every variable typed and scaled, univariate summaries, one bivariate correlation, box-plots, and outlier analysis — all computed and plotted by you, raw sheet attached.","support":"~400 words on your collection method and what surprised you.","llc":"Show the raw data sheet to the cohort and defend one statistical choice under questions."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Outlier Hunt","primary":"Find a real dataset with genuine extreme values you can name (a fee ledger, a sensor log, exam marks). Detect outliers using box-plot plus trimmed and Winsorised means, then INVESTIGATE each extreme — is it a data-entry error or a real event? Bring evidence for every verdict.","support":"~400 words on how each decision would change a real conclusion.","llc":"Present one outlier and walk the cohort through the consequence of trusting versus removing it."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Two Things That Move Together","primary":"Pick two real variables you measure over at least 30 units, compute Pearson and rank correlation, and fit BOTH regression lines (X on Y and Y on X). Prove with your own reasoning whether the link is causal or spurious.","support":"~400 words tracing your data collection and the correlation-vs-causation logic.","llc":"Defend your causal claim while the cohort proposes rival explanations you must rule out."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Predicting Next Week","primary":"Take a real time-ordered dataset you source, fit a regression to predict a future value, then WAIT for reality and record the actual outcome; compute and interpret your prediction error. Bring prediction and actual side by side.","support":"~400 words on why the error was the size it was.","llc":"Show predicted vs actual and explain the gap without blaming 'randomness'."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Chart That Changed a Decision","primary":"Build a set of hand-and-software visualisations of a real dataset that a real named person (a shopkeeper, warden, or coach) will actually use to make one decision. Interview them after they decide.","support":"~400 words on which chart form communicated best and why.","llc":"Present the chart and report the real decision it drove, defending your design under questions."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAC01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAC02 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Choose ONE real data source you can access (a fitness app's export, a shop's transaction log, a public open-data CSV) and dissect it against the data taxonomy — numeric/categorical/graphical/high-dimensional and its source type (time-series, transactional, spatial, social-network).","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the raw source + a labelled breakdown of its data types and source category. Write 3-4 sentences on which characteristic makes this data hard to analyse and why the textbook label fits (or doesn't)."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Find and interview ONE real person who works with data in their job — an accountant, a lab technician, a bank clerk, a small-business owner using Excel. Map their actual daily work onto the data-science-vs-statistics/database/ML/programming distinctions.","deliverable_notes":"The person's name + verifiable contact (with consent) + a one-line quote about their work. Write 3-4 sentences placing their role on the data-science skills map and naming one thing the textbook definition gets wrong about real practice."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Pick a real app or platform you personally use (name it) and document how it exhibits the 6 Vs of big data on YOU — what it stores, how often, in what formats — then identify one 'big data myth' it markets.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots evidencing the data it holds on you + a 6-Vs table + the myth you spotted. Write 3-4 sentences answering: who is harmed if this data collection succeeds asymmetrically, and what would you change to protect the user?"},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Import a real dataset (at least 3 columns, 100+ rows) that you obtained yourself into R. Build a data frame, clean one messy column, and produce at least two ggplot2 visualisations.","deliverable_notes":"Your runnable R script + a screenshot of the two plots + the console output. Write 3-4 sentences on the cleaning step and what the visualisation revealed that a summary table would have hidden."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Using R on a real dataset, compute central tendency, dispersion, and run ONE hypothesis test that answers a genuine question tied to a use case (does a discount move purchases? does a policy offer differ by group?).","deliverable_notes":"The R output for the statistics and test + your stated hypothesis. Write 3-4 sentences integrating the number with the real decision: what would a business actually do differently given your p-value and effect size?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"My First Real Data Pipeline in R","primary":"Source a real dataset (3+ columns, 100+ rows) you obtained yourself and write R code that imports, cleans, computes central-tendency and dispersion, runs one hypothesis test, and produces at least two ggplot2 visualisations. The script must run live end to end.","support":"~400 words on the data-leakage or cleaning traps you caught.","llc":"Run your script live in front of the cohort and defend one cleaning decision when challenged."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The 6 Vs of a Real App","primary":"Pick a real app you use, name it, and document with concrete evidence (screenshots, update frequencies, formats) how it exhibits each of the 6 Vs of big data — then bust one 'big data myth' it markets.","support":"~400 words on the privacy trade-off you found.","llc":"Present the app's data footprint on you and defend the one myth you claim to have debunked."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Day in a Data Worker's Life","primary":"Shadow or interview a real named person who works with data in their job (consent, verifiable contact) and map their actual workflow onto the data-science-vs-statistics/database/ML framework and the DS roles-and-skills model.","support":"~400 words on where theory met reality.","llc":"Quote them and tell the cohort what the textbook got wrong about their job, taking questions."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"360 View — and Its Shadow","primary":"With one real consenting person, assemble a mock '360 view of the customer' from data they let you gather across sources, then write the privacy risk register for what you assembled. Bring the profile plus the risks.","support":"~400 words on where you drew the ethical line and why.","llc":"Defend your line while the cohort argues you went too far or not far enough."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"R Notebook for a Real Decision","primary":"Partner with a real small operation (a shop, club, or hostel mess) and build an R analysis answering ONE question they genuinely have, using their real data. Deliver the runnable notebook plus their reaction.","support":"~400 words on how you translated a vague ask into a testable question.","llc":"Demo the notebook live and report whether it actually helped them."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAC02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAC03 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Find a real situation that produces a system of linear equations — scaling a recipe for a known headcount, a small mixture/alloy problem, currents in a simple circuit you draw, or a traffic-flow junction you observe. Solve it BY HAND using Gaussian elimination, then verify with code.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-worked elimination steps (photo) + a short script that confirms the solution + a line describing the real situation. Write 3-4 sentences on what a row-exchange or a near-zero pivot meant physically in your problem."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Build a real data matrix yourself (a small term-document table from 5 texts you pick, or a features table you collect) and compute its rank and null space to find redundant/dependent columns.","deliverable_notes":"The matrix + your rank and any null-space vector + the code. Write 3-4 sentences integrating the algebra with the data: which columns turned out redundant, and what does 'this column space has dimension r' mean for the information actually in your data?"},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Choose ONE determinant property (e.g., det(AB)=det(A)det(B), or that row operations scale the determinant predictably) and construct your own proof for the 2x2 or 3x3 case, plus a numerical instance you build that illustrates each step.","deliverable_notes":"Your handwritten proof (photo) + the worked numerical example. Write 3-4 sentences on where you got stuck constructing the argument and what unstuck you — name the reasoning move you now trust yourself to repeat."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Log a real repeating process into a transition matrix — weather state at your location for 20+ days, or which mess counter is busy hour-to-hour, or app-to-app switching on your phone. Compute its eigenvalues and the dominant eigenvector by hand and by code.","deliverable_notes":"Your logged data + the transition matrix + the dominant eigenvector. Write 3-4 sentences interpreting the dominant eigenvector as the long-run behaviour of YOUR process, and say whether it matches what you actually observe."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Take a photograph you shot yourself, convert it to a grayscale matrix, compute its SVD in code, and reconstruct rank-k approximations for several k values.","deliverable_notes":"The original photo + your rank-5, rank-20, rank-50 (or similar) reconstructions + a plot of singular-value decay. Write 3-4 sentences on where the 'elbow' sits and what that says about how much real information the image actually holds."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Solver I Wrote Myself","primary":"Implement Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting FROM SCRATCH — no linear-algebra library for the core solve. Test it on a real ill-conditioned system you construct and compare its answer and stability against a library solver. Code runs live.","support":"~400 words on why pivoting mattered for your system.","llc":"Run your solver on a system the cohort hands you and defend your pivoting strategy under questions."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Eigen-Anything","primary":"Apply eigen-decomposition or PCA — computed on your own covariance matrix, not a black box — to a real dataset you collected (faces you photograph with consent, handwritten digits you scan, sensor logs). Show the principal components and a reconstruction.","support":"~400 words on how many components you kept and why.","llc":"Demo the reconstruction and defend your component count against a challenge to keep more or fewer."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Compressing a Real Image with SVD","primary":"Photograph something yourself, compute its SVD, and produce rank-k approximations; plot the singular-value decay and quantify compression ratio versus visual quality. Bring the original plus the compressions.","support":"~400 words on the maths of why low rank captures most of the image.","llc":"Show the image degrading as k drops and explain where the elbow is and why."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Markov Chain of a Real Process","primary":"Log a real repeating process (weather for 30 days, mess-counter traffic, phone app-switching) into a transition matrix, find its stationary distribution via the dominant eigenvector, and verify it by simulating the chain.","support":"~400 words connecting the eigenvector to the long-run prediction.","llc":"Defend your stationary prediction while the cohort proposes what-if changes to the transitions."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Proof I Own","primary":"Choose one non-trivial theorem (rank-nullity, the spectral theorem for symmetric matrices, or det(AB)=det(A)det(B)) and write a COMPLETE proof in your own hand, plus a worked numerical instance you construct illustrating each step.","support":"~400 words on where you got stuck and how you broke through.","llc":"Prove it live at the board and take questions on every step."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAC03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDACP01 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Design a small real schema (a club roster, a lab-equipment log) and implement its constraints, then write nested queries that answer questions the flat data couldn't.","deliverable_notes":"The CREATE TABLE with constraints + two nested queries and their output on your real-ish data. Write 3-4 sentences on one constraint that rejected a realistic bad row and why the nested query beat a simpler one."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-4","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"On a larger table you populate, apply partitioning and build views that join multiple tables into the shape a real user actually wants to read.","deliverable_notes":"The partitioned table + the view definitions + a query against the view. Write 3-4 sentences integrating the two: how did partitioning change performance, and what real reporting need does your view make one-line simple?"},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 5-6","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Write a stored function/procedure and a trigger that enforce a real rule, keeping a log of every PL/SQL compile error and how you resolved it.","deliverable_notes":"The working procedure + trigger + your error-and-fix log. Write 3-4 sentences on the most confusing compile/runtime error and what it taught you about how PL/SQL executes that the syntax alone didn't reveal."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 7-8","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Build a form/report front end and a cursor-driven report with exception handling over your real-ish data.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the form/report + the cursor code handling at least one exception. Write 3-4 sentences on the edge case your exception handler catches and what the report would output wrongly without it."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 9","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Assemble the pieces into a small working application and put it in front of ONE real person who would actually use it; watch them try it.","deliverable_notes":"A short demo transcript + the user's name (consent) + one thing they got stuck on. Write 3-4 sentences on the gap between what you built and what a real user expected — what would you change after watching them?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Working Database App","primary":"Build a complete small database application — schema, constraints, procedures, triggers, and a form/report front end — for a real need you identify (club membership, lab inventory, personal finance). It must run and accept real input.","support":"~400 words on the design decisions.","llc":"Demo the app doing a real transaction live and defend one constraint under questions."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Triggers That Enforce a Real Rule","primary":"Pick a real business rule (no overdraw, no double-booking, audit-on-change) and implement it as triggers plus constraints; prove enforcement by attempting violations.","support":"~400 words on which rule each guard encodes.","llc":"Attempt a violation live and show it blocked, explaining the damage it would have caused."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Report on a Cursor","primary":"Build a real reporting need using cursors, procedures, and exception handling over real-ish data; the report must be correct and handle edge cases.","support":"~400 words on your exception paths.","llc":"Run it live and explain how each exception path keeps the output trustworthy."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Query Optimizer's Apprentice","primary":"Take a real slow-query need (large table, nested or partitioned) and make it fast — bring before-and-after execution plans and timings you measured yourself.","support":"~400 words on what the plan revealed.","llc":"Show the plan change live and defend your indexing or partitioning choice."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Data Integrity Under Attack","primary":"Design a schema whose constraints and triggers survive a battery of bad inputs you throw at it; bring the attack log and every rejection.","support":"~400 words on the failure modes you defended.","llc":"Run the attack live and narrate how each defense fires."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDACP01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDACP02 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-5","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"In SciLab or R, implement matrix operations, eigenvalues/eigenvectors, equation solving, and reduced-row-echelon form on a real matrix you build (from a small dataset or a real system).","deliverable_notes":"Your code + the outputs for eigen, solve, and RREF on YOUR matrix. Write 3-4 sentences interpreting one result physically — what does the largest eigenvalue or the solution vector mean for the real thing your matrix represents?"},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 6-7","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Plot real functions and their derivatives, and build a frequency-table representation of a real dataset you collected, in R/SciLab.","deliverable_notes":"The function plots + the frequency table and its chart + the raw data. Write 3-4 sentences on what the derivative plot or the frequency shape tells you about the underlying real quantity that the raw numbers alone don't."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 8-9","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Detect outliers in a real dataset, then compare TWO real mutually-exclusive risky options someone actually faces (two investments, suppliers, or crop choices) using statistical risk measures.","deliverable_notes":"The outlier detection output + the risk comparison of the two real options. Write 3-4 sentences on who actually bears the risk of the option you'd recommend, and whether one outlier could flip your recommendation."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 10-11","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Run regression diagnostics and a correlation analysis on real data, deliberately checking whether the model's assumptions actually hold rather than trusting the fit.","deliverable_notes":"The residual/diagnostic plots + the correlation output. Write 3-4 sentences on which assumption you found shakiest and how you'll routinely check it before believing a regression in future — what's your new habit?"},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 12-13","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Collect a real time series (30+ points you log yourself) and fit both a time-series model and a linear regression to forecast the next values.","deliverable_notes":"The logged series + both fitted models + the forecast. Write 3-4 sentences integrating the two approaches: which forecast do you trust for YOUR data and what feature of the real series (trend, seasonality) decided it?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"From Raw Data to a Defended Model","primary":"Source a real dataset and, in R or SPSS, build the full chain — clean, visualise, detect outliers, correlate, fit a regression WITH diagnostics — and defend that the model's assumptions actually hold. Output runs.","support":"~400 words on the assumption checks.","llc":"Run the diagnostics live and defend one assumption when a cohort member says it's violated."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Risky Decision, Quantified","primary":"Find two real mutually-exclusive options someone actually faces (two investments, suppliers, crops) and use statistical risk measures on real data to recommend one.","support":"~400 words on how you modelled the risk.","llc":"Present the numbers and defend the recommendation to a skeptic who prefers the other option."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Forecasting Something Real","primary":"Collect a real time series (30+ points you log — prices, temperature, footfall), fit a model, forecast the next values, then WAIT and record the actuals; report your error.","support":"~400 words on why the error was its size.","llc":"Show forecast versus reality and explain the gap without invoking 'randomness'."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Linear Algebra That Solves a Real Problem","primary":"Find a real problem that reduces to a linear system or eigenproblem (mixture, network flow, ranking) and solve it in SciLab or R, verifying the solution against reality.","support":"~400 words on the modelling step.","llc":"Demo the solve and interpret the eigen or solution physically under questions."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Outlier or Truth?","primary":"Take a real dataset with extreme values, apply outlier-detection methods, and INVESTIGATE each extreme to rule error versus genuine event; bring evidence for each verdict.","support":"~400 words on your decision criteria.","llc":"Present one extreme and defend keep-or-drop when the cohort argues the opposite."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDACP02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDACP03 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Set up a Hadoop environment yourself and complete file-management tasks on HDFS, logging every error and fix along the way.","deliverable_notes":"The terminal transcript of HDFS commands succeeding + your error-and-fix diary. Write 3-4 sentences on the hardest setup failure and what it revealed about how HDFS actually stores and blocks data underneath the commands."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-4","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Write and run MapReduce programs to mine real data — a weather dataset and a stock-count task — on data you source.","deliverable_notes":"The mapper/reducer code + the run + a sample of the output for both jobs. Write 3-4 sentences on what the reduce phase aggregated and one result you verified against an independent source to prove correctness."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 5","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Build Pig Latin scripts that load, transform, and aggregate a real messy dataset across multiple stages.","deliverable_notes":"The Pig script + each stage's output. Write 3-4 sentences integrating the stages: describe the dataset transforming through the pipeline and which relational operator most changed its shape."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 6-8","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Run MongoDB operations (CRUD, a MapReduce aggregation) and HBase commands on real data you load into each.","deliverable_notes":"The command transcripts + query results from both MongoDB and HBase. Write 3-4 sentences on one query that was natural in the document/column store but would be awkward in a relational table, and why."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 9-10","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Collect a real dataset (wait-times, defect counts, arrivals) and fit candidate probability distributions to it in R and in SPSS, testing goodness-of-fit.","deliverable_notes":"The fitted distributions + the goodness-of-fit output from both tools + the raw data. Write 3-4 sentences on which distribution best matched YOUR real data and what real-world mechanism would explain why that distribution arises."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Distributed Job I Ran","primary":"Stand up Hadoop yourself, then run a real MapReduce job on a dataset of at least 100MB you sourced (weather, stock, logs) end to end; bring code, terminal transcript, and output.","support":"~400 words on the setup failures you fixed.","llc":"Show the run (live or recorded) and explain the map versus reduce phases to the cohort."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"One Dataset Across the NoSQL Zoo","primary":"Load one real dataset into MongoDB AND HBase, run equivalent queries, and compare with a MapReduce aggregation; bring all three plus timings.","support":"~400 words on which store fit the data.","llc":"Defend which store you'd choose for this data when the cohort argues for another."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Probability Fitted to Reality","primary":"Collect a real dataset (wait-times, defect counts, arrivals) and fit candidate probability distributions in R and SPSS; test goodness-of-fit and pick the best with evidence.","support":"~400 words on why that distribution arises for this process.","llc":"Present the fit and defend your chosen distribution against a rival candidate."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Pig Pipeline on Messy Data","primary":"Take a real messy dataset and build a Pig Latin pipeline that cleans and aggregates it, with each stage verifiable. Runs live.","support":"~400 words on why Pig suited the transformation.","llc":"Run it live and explain one operator's effect on the data when questioned."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Weather/Stock Miner","primary":"Mine a real public weather or stock dataset with MapReduce to answer one genuine question (hottest month, most volatile stock), then verify the answer against an independent source.","support":"~400 words on the job design.","llc":"Present the finding and defend its correctness against the independent check."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDACP03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDACP04 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"In Python, load a real dataset you sourced with pandas/numpy, inspect it, and clean at least two real problems in it (missing values, wrong types, duplicates).","deliverable_notes":"Your runnable notebook + before/after of the cleaned columns + the raw source. Write 3-4 sentences on the messiest real-data problem you hit and why a model trained on the uncleaned version would have quietly gone wrong."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-4","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Build a classification and a regression model in scikit-learn on a real dataset, using a proper train/test split, and report honest metrics.","deliverable_notes":"The code + the confusion matrix / error metrics + the split you used. Write 3-4 sentences on which feature the model leaned on most and whether that makes real-world sense or hints at leakage."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 5-6","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Apply PCA to reduce a real high-dimensional dataset, then cluster the reduced data with K-means and hierarchical clustering, comparing the groupings.","deliverable_notes":"The variance-explained plot + the cluster visualisation + code. Write 3-4 sentences integrating reduction and clustering: did compressing first make the clusters cleaner, and what real structure do the clusters correspond to?"},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 7-8","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Build and train a small neural network in Keras/PyTorch on a real dataset, keeping a tuning diary of what you changed (layers, learning rate, epochs) and its effect.","deliverable_notes":"The training code + the loss curve + your tuning diary. Write 3-4 sentences on the change that most improved (or worsened) learning and what the loss curve taught you about overfitting on YOUR data."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 9-10","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Evaluate a model you trained and audit it for one concrete harm — bias across a group, or a high-cost error type — on real data.","deliverable_notes":"The evaluation output + evidence of the harm (disparate error rates, a costly misclassification). Write 3-4 sentences on who is harmed if this model ships as-is, and one change you tested to reduce that harm."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The ML Model I Built in Python","primary":"Source or collect a real dataset and, in Python, build a full ML pipeline — clean, engineer features, train at least two models, cross-validate, and report metrics. Code runs live.","support":"~400 words on the overfitting or leakage you caught.","llc":"Run inference live on a fresh case and defend your metric choice under questions."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Neural Net From Near-Scratch","primary":"Build and train a small neural network on a real dataset (images or tabular), explain every layer, and verify it learns via the loss curve. Runs live.","support":"~400 words on the tuning ordeal.","llc":"Train one epoch live and read the loss curve for the cohort, taking questions on your architecture."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Bias in My Model","primary":"Train a model on real data, audit it for bias across a sensitive attribute you name, quantify disparate errors, and test one mitigation you implement.","support":"~400 words on the source of the bias.","llc":"Present the bias evidence and defend the mitigation against 'it just moves the problem'."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Clusters From Real Behaviour","primary":"Collect real behavioural data (survey, usage, sensor), apply PCA plus clustering in Python, profile the clusters, and validate one by interviewing a real member.","support":"~400 words on choosing k and validating.","llc":"Defend your k and report what the interviewee said about their cluster."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"ML on a Problem Someone Has","primary":"Find a real person or group with a prediction need and build a Python solution on their real data, delivered as a runnable notebook they can use.","support":"~400 words on turning their vague need into a modellable target.","llc":"Demo it solving their problem live and take unscripted questions on where it would fail."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDACP04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAE01 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Find real sparse data (an adjacency table of who-knows-whom in your class, or a term-document matrix from a few texts) and implement BOTH a dense array and a sparse representation for it; measure the space each uses.","deliverable_notes":"Your code + the two memory footprints you measured + the sparsity percentage of your real data. Write 3-4 sentences on the crossover point: at what density does the sparse representation stop being worth it for your data?"},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Implement a stack- or queue-backed feature that mimics something real you use — an expression evaluator, an undo history, a browser back-button, or a print queue — from scratch.","deliverable_notes":"The runnable code + a short demo transcript (inputs and outputs). Write 3-4 sentences on why a stack/queue is the right structure here and what would break if you used the wrong one (e.g., undo with a queue)."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Implement at least four sorting algorithms yourself, then race them on real datasets of growing size you generate or source; plot runtime against n.","deliverable_notes":"Your runtime-vs-n plots + the code. Write 3-4 sentences on where your MEASURED curves diverged from the textbook Big-O — name one case where the 'slower' algorithm won on small or nearly-sorted real data, and what that taught you about trusting theory blindly."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Collect real 2D points you can name (GPS of shops you visit, or pixel coordinates from an image) and build a KD-tree for nearest-neighbour queries; compare its speed to brute-force search.","deliverable_notes":"The code + your point data + the two timings. Write 3-4 sentences on how balanced your tree came out on real (non-uniform) data and how that affected query speed versus the ideal."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Model a real network you can verify — campus walkways you measure, bus routes, or a friendship graph you collect with consent — as a graph, then run shortest-path and minimum-spanning-tree algorithms on it.","deliverable_notes":"The graph (drawn) + the algorithm output + one result checked against reality. Write 3-4 sentences integrating graph theory with the real map: did the shortest path match the route people actually take, and if not, what does the graph miss?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Data Structure Under a Real Feature","primary":"Pick a real feature you use (autocomplete, undo, map routing, a leaderboard) and implement the data structure that powers it FROM SCRATCH — trie, stack, graph, or heap — with your own code, tested on real inputs.","support":"~400 words on the structure versus its alternatives for this feature.","llc":"Demo the feature and defend the structure choice under questions about scale and edge cases."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Sort Race","primary":"Implement at least four sorting algorithms yourself and race them on real datasets of growing size you generate or source; plot runtime versus n and empirically fit the complexity class. Bring the plots and code.","support":"~400 words on where theory and measurement diverged.","llc":"Run a live race and explain an anomaly the cohort points out."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Graph of Something Real","primary":"Build a graph from a real network you can name (campus walkways you measure, a consented friendship graph, bus routes) and implement shortest-path plus minimum-spanning-tree; verify one result against reality by walking the path.","support":"~400 words on modelling choices (weights, direction).","llc":"Show the map, run the algorithm live, and defend the route when someone claims a better one."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Nearest Neighbour in the Real World","primary":"Collect real 2D/3D points (GPS of places you visit, image features) and build a KD-tree for nearest-neighbour queries; compare speed against brute force on the same data. Code runs.","support":"~400 words on how real, clustered data affected tree balance.","llc":"Query live and explain why the tree's balance helped or hurt."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Complexity I Measured Myself","primary":"Take three operations you implement (search, insert, delete) on two structures and empirically measure their complexity on real inputs of growing size, then reconcile the curves with Big-O theory. Bring data and code.","support":"~400 words on the reconciliation.","llc":"Defend one measured curve against its theoretical class when it doesn't quite match."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAE01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAE03 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Find a real operation that currently stores data in a flat way — a shop's notebook, a spreadsheet a club maintains, a diary of readings — and argue file-system-versus-DBMS for THIS case with concrete evidence of the pain a DBMS would remove.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of the real flat storage + a short list of the specific problems (redundancy, no concurrency, no integrity) it suffers. Write 3-4 sentences on the one transaction or query this operation cannot safely do today that a DBMS would make trivial."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Interview a real operator (a shopkeeper, clinic receptionist, club secretary) about how their work really flows, and model their world as an ER diagram — entities, attributes, relationships, cardinalities — then show it back to them.","deliverable_notes":"The ER diagram + the operator's name (consent) + their correction quoted. Write 3-4 sentences on one entity or relationship you got wrong until they corrected you — what did the real domain expert know that your first model missed?"},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Translate that ER model into a normalised relational schema and define the real integrity constraints their domain demands (a fee can't be negative, a booking can't overlap).","deliverable_notes":"The CREATE TABLE statements with keys and constraints + a mapping note from ER to tables. Write 3-4 sentences integrating design and rules: name one constraint that encodes a real-world law of their business, and what bad data it silently blocks."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Build the database with real-ish data and write at least six SQL queries answering the operator's actual questions, plus one trigger that enforces a rule automatically.","deliverable_notes":"The queries with their output + the trigger firing on a test insert. Write 3-4 sentences on the hardest query (a nested or aggregate one) and how you verified its answer was actually correct, not just plausible."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Implement one piece of logic BOTH as a PL/SQL procedure with cursors/exceptions AND as an operation in a NoSQL store, then compare the two paradigms.","deliverable_notes":"Both implementations + a short side-by-side of code length and behaviour. Write 3-4 sentences on what learning the second paradigm taught you about the first — what did you assume was 'just how databases work' that turned out to be a relational-only choice?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Database for a Real Business","primary":"Interview a real operator (shop, clinic, club, mess), model their world as an ER diagram, build the normalised schema, load real-ish data, and write at least eight queries answering their actual questions plus one trigger enforcing a real rule.","support":"~400 words on the ER-to-relational decisions.","llc":"Run their top query live and defend one normalisation choice against a 'why not one big table' challenge."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Query They Couldn't Answer","primary":"Find a real dataset or organisation with a question their current tools can't answer, then build the schema and SQL that answers it, with proof the answer is correct.","support":"~400 words on why the question was hard for their existing setup.","llc":"Run it live and take questions on edge cases like nulls and duplicates that could corrupt the answer."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Constraints That Caught a Bug","primary":"Design integrity constraints and triggers for a real schema, then deliberately feed bad data to prove each one fires; bring the rejection logs.","support":"~400 words naming the real-world error each constraint prevents.","llc":"Attempt a bad insert live and show it blocked, explaining what would have happened without the guard."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"SQL vs NoSQL, Same Problem","primary":"Model one real dataset both relationally (with PL/SQL logic) and in a NoSQL store; run equivalent operations and compare code, performance, and fit.","support":"~400 words on which paradigm suited the data's shape.","llc":"Defend which one you'd actually ship for this data when the cohort argues the other way."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Report That Runs on a Cursor","primary":"Build a real reporting need (a shop's monthly summary, a club's dues status) as a PL/SQL program using cursors, procedures, and exception handling, running on real-ish data. Code runs.","support":"~400 words on your exception-handling design.","llc":"Run the report live and explain how each exception path protects the output."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAE03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAE06 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Pick a real website you use (name it) and map its analytics needs onto the Web Analytics 2.0 framework — clickstream, outcomes, experimentation, voice-of-customer, competitive intelligence.","deliverable_notes":"A one-page map of the site against the five 2.0 pillars, with a concrete example for each. Write 3-4 sentences on which pillar this particular site most neglects and what it's flying blind about as a result."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Instrument a real page (your own blog/portfolio/club site, or the public Google Analytics demo account) and pull the eight critical web metrics with real numbers.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the eight metrics with actual values + the source named. Write 3-4 sentences on the one metric that most surprised you and what real visitor behaviour it exposed."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For a real site, define its macro and micro conversions, then run a short qualitative survey (5+ real respondents) and integrate what they say with the clickstream numbers.","deliverable_notes":"The conversion definitions + the survey responses + one number their words explain. Write 3-4 sentences integrating the two data types: where did the qualitative 'why' contradict or complete the quantitative 'what'?"},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Run a genuine A/B test on a real page you control (two headlines or CTAs) with real visitors, tracking how tempted you are to stop early when a variant looks ahead.","deliverable_notes":"Both variants + the visitor counts + your final honest conclusion. Write 3-4 sentences on the peeking/false-positive trap: at what point did you WANT to call it, was that sample size enough, and how did you keep yourself honest?"},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Analyse a real social/mobile/video account's performance (your own, or one you get access to) across its engagement metrics and reflect on the human side of 'engagement'.","deliverable_notes":"The metrics pulled + the top-performing piece identified. Write 3-4 sentences on what 'maximising engagement' actually does to the creator and the audience — is the metric serving the humans, or are the humans serving the metric?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Site I Instrumented","primary":"Set up analytics on a real web page you build or control, drive real traffic, and produce a report with the eight critical metrics, a conversion funnel, and one actionable recommendation grounded in the data.","support":"~400 words on what the numbers revealed about real behaviour.","llc":"Show the live dashboard and defend your recommendation when the cohort proposes a different fix."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"An A/B Test With Real Visitors","primary":"Design and run a genuine A/B test on a real page (two headlines or CTAs), collect real visitor data, and reach a statistically honest conclusion reporting your sample size, effect, and uncertainty.","support":"~400 words on the peeking and false-positive traps you resisted.","llc":"Present both variants and the verdict, defending your statistics against a 'you stopped too early' challenge."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Decoding a Competitor","primary":"Pick a real named website and run a competitive-intelligence teardown using public tools (traffic estimates, keyword and search analysis, audience segmentation) with cited evidence, then propose where you'd beat them.","support":"~400 words on what the public signals do and don't reveal.","llc":"Present the teardown and defend one strategic bet under questioning."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Voice of the Real Customer","primary":"Run a real voice-of-customer exercise for a real site or product (15+ real respondents) and integrate the qualitative findings with the quantitative clickstream to explain ONE specific behaviour.","support":"~400 words on where words and numbers agreed or clashed.","llc":"Quote a respondent and show the metric their words explained, defending the link."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Engagement Autopsy","primary":"Analyse a real social or video account's performance across metrics, find what actually drives engagement with evidence, and write the attention-economy trade-off it creates.","support":"~400 words on the driver you isolated.","llc":"Present the engagement driver and defend it against a rival hypothesis the cohort raises."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAE06'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PDAE07 · m-sc-computer-science-data-analytics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Choose a real social account or topic and catalogue every data source available for it, then classify each source as estimated versus factual data.","deliverable_notes":"A table of the sources with the estimated/factual tag and how you know + the account/topic named. Write 3-4 sentences on the one source most people would wrongly trust as 'factual' and the risk of building an analysis on it."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Use a real social analytics tool (native platform insights or a free third-party tool) on a real account and map its interaction patterns — who engages, how content flows, the two-way channel behaviour.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of the tool's output + a sketch of the interaction pattern you observed. Write 3-4 sentences on what the real interaction data showed about the account that its follower count alone completely hides."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Run the full analytics cycle on a real account's data — analysis, insight, investigation — combining default platform metrics with at least one custom metric you define.","deliverable_notes":"The metrics (default + your custom one, with its formula) + the insight you reached. Write 3-4 sentences integrating them: what did your custom metric capture that the default dashboard missed, and why did the account need it?"},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Build a dashboard and short report FOR a real named stakeholder (a club, a small business, a creator) from their real social data, then get their feedback on whether it helps them decide.","deliverable_notes":"The dashboard (screenshot) + the stakeholder's name (consent) + their feedback quoted. Write 3-4 sentences on how you had to change the report so a non-analyst could act on it — what did you cut, and what did they actually look at first?"},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Design a social-media strategy for a real cause or account and examine the ethics of its influence — who is being moved, is the persuasion transparent, who benefits.","deliverable_notes":"The strategy outline + a named example of a persuasion tactic it uses. Write 3-4 sentences answering: is this influence fair to the audience, who could be harmed if it works, and where would you refuse to go even if it boosted the metrics?"}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Account I Analyzed for Real","primary":"Get access to (or run) a real social account and produce a full analytics report — data sources, the analysis cycle, default plus custom metrics, and three evidence-backed insights using real numbers only.","support":"~400 words on the estimated-vs-factual judgements you made.","llc":"Present the insights and defend one custom metric against 'the platform already shows this'."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Dashboard a Real Person Uses","primary":"Build a live social-media dashboard for a real named stakeholder from their real data; they use it and give feedback. Bring the dashboard and their reaction.","support":"~400 words on designing for a non-analyst decision-maker.","llc":"Demo it and report what the stakeholder actually changed because of it, taking questions on your metric choices."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Strategy, Then Proof","primary":"Design a social-media strategy for a real account with measurable goals, run it for at least two weeks, and measure whether it worked against a baseline. Bring before-and-after data.","support":"~400 words on what you'd do differently.","llc":"Defend whether the strategy earned its cost when the cohort argues the change was just noise."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Estimated vs Factual","primary":"For one real topic or account, gather the same metric from at least three sources (native analytics versus third-party estimators) and quantify how much they disagree; determine which to trust and why.","support":"~400 words on the sources' methods.","llc":"Present the disagreement and defend your trust ranking under challenge."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Influence Audit","primary":"Analyse a real campaign or account's influence (a political, brand, or cause account you name) with real engagement data, and write the ethics of its persuasion — who is moved, is it transparent, who benefits.","support":"~400 words on the mechanics of the influence.","llc":"Present the influence mechanics and defend where you'd draw the ethical line."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PDAE07'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENC01 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Read aloud one pilgrim-portrait (the Pardoner, the Nun, the Friar, or the Doctor) from Chaucer's General Prologue in reconstructed Middle English pronunciation — sound the final -e, open the long vowels. Use the Riverside/Oxford text.","deliverable_notes":"Your own 30–60 second audio/video of you reading 12+ lines, plus a photo of your working copy hand-marked for stress and sounded final -e, your hand in frame. Write 3–4 sentences: which words sound nothing like their modern form, and what the estates-satire portrait quietly exposes about that pilgrim's corruption."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Take the twin-compasses conceit in Donne's 'A Valediction: Forbidding Mourning' (or the conceit in 'The Canonization'). Reconstruct it as a labelled hand-drawn diagram — draw the compasses and pin each of the final stanzas' lines to a part of the geometry.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-drawn labelled diagram + 3–4 sentences naming the exact line where the geometric analogy stops being literal and becomes emotional over-reach, and whether the conceit finally convinces you or strains."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Set Satan's temptation of Eve (Paradise Lost, Book IX, ll. 679–732) beside the speaker's seduction argument in Marvell's 'To His Coy Mistress'. In your own hand, mark the shared persuasive structure — flattery, false logic, urgency.","deliverable_notes":"A two-column annotated sheet with your margin arrows linking specific matching lines across the two poems + 3–4 sentences on the one rhetorical move Milton's Satan makes that Marvell's speaker never risks."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Crack Dryden's 'Absalom and Achitophel' as topical allegory. Using a scholarly edition's notes, build your OWN key mapping David / Absalom / Achitophel to the real 1680s figures (Charles II / Monmouth / Shaftesbury), logging each step of how you resolved an allusion you did not recognise.","deliverable_notes":"Your handwritten allegory-key + a short method-log (how you moved from 'I don't know this name' to a resolved identification) + 3–4 sentences on what decoding taught you about reading topical satire without footnotes."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Choose ONE elegy of mortality from Unit V — Owen's 'Anthem for Doomed Youth', Dylan Thomas's 'Do Not Go Gentle into That Good Night', or Auden's elegy for Yeats. Read it aloud at the pace of real grief, timing your pauses; then hand-copy it and mark every sound (half-rhyme, assonance, refrain) that carries the mourning.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + the hand-copied, sound-marked text + 3–4 sentences on one technical choice (Owen's pararhyme, Thomas's villanelle refrain) and how it makes the grief land differently than a paraphrase ever could."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Poem in My Own Mouth","primary":"Choose one prescribed poem; record yourself reading the full text aloud and produce a complete hand-marked scansion of its metre with your marginal close-reading in your own hand — not a typed essay.","support":"~400 words on how speaking the poem changed your reading of it.","llc":"Recite a passage live to the cohort and defend one scansion choice under unscripted questioning. AI can describe the metre; it cannot voice YOUR reading or mark YOUR page."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Two Persuaders, One Argument","primary":"Build an original comparative close-reading of two persuasion poems (Marvell + Donne, or Milton's Satan + Dryden) as a hand-annotated dossier — your marginalia and linking arrows, not prose.","support":"~400 words on the shared rhetoric and where it diverges.","llc":"Present the linked lines live and take an unscripted challenge to your comparison."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Decoding the Age","primary":"Take Dryden, Gray or Burns and produce your OWN annotated edition of 40 lines — every allusion resolved by you with a cited source, handwritten.","support":"~400 words on what the poet assumed his readers already knew.","llc":"Hand the cohort one un-footnoted line and walk them through your decoding live, answering their questions."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Sound Made Visible","primary":"Take one modern poem (Hughes's 'Hawk Roosting', Larkin's 'Whitsun Weddings', or Boland's 'Achilles Woman') and produce a hand-drawn 'sound map' of the whole poem — colour-coded stress, caesura, rhyme — plus your recorded reading that matches the map.","support":"~400 words on how the sound builds the meaning.","llc":"Play your reading and defend one mapped choice."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Manuscript to the Page","primary":"Pick Chaucer's General Prologue; compare a manuscript/facsimile image of 20 lines to a modern edition, hand-transcribe the Middle English yourself, and mark every editorial change (spelling, punctuation, glossing).","support":"~400 words on what modern editing hides or smooths away.","llc":"Read your transcription aloud in Middle English and answer questions on your editorial choices."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENC01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENC02 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Stage the desertion of Everyman — the moment Fellowship, Kindred and Goods abandon him when Death summons. Block a 2–3 minute scene with two classmates so the allegorical figures physically walk away from Everyman one by one.","deliverable_notes":"A recorded video of your blocked scene + your hand-drawn blocking chart (where each allegory stands, and the exact exit each takes) + 3–4 sentences on what staging the abstraction taught you that reading the personification on the page did not."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Perform ONE aside from Marlowe's Barabas (The Jew of Malta) or Jonson's Volpone to camera. Apply the mechanics of the aside: break the fourth wall, let the audience in on the con, then snap back into the scene.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded delivery of the aside straight to camera + a marked script showing exactly where you turned out and where you turned back + 3–4 sentences on how the aside makes the audience complicit in the villain's scheme."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Take Vittoria's arraignment speech from Webster's The White Devil. Rehearse and deliver it as a woman defending herself before a hostile, rigged court — find the human being under the 'white devil' label.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded performance + a hand-annotated script marking where you chose defiance and where fear leaks through + 3–4 sentences on what the scene asks us to feel about a guilty woman who is given no fair hearing."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Read the proviso scene of Congreve's The Way of the World beside a scene of Synge's The Playboy of the Western World. In your hand, mark how each play's LANGUAGE encodes its society — urbane Restoration wit versus the Irish peasant idiom.","deliverable_notes":"A two-column annotated sheet linking specific lines to the society they come from + 3–4 sentences on what each idiom lets its characters say that the other's could not."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Stage the opening of Beckett's Waiting for Godot, or a menacing exchange from Pinter's The Birthday Party. Time the silences with a stopwatch and honour them exactly — do not rush the pauses.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded 2-minute performance with real timed pauses + a log of the exact pause durations you chose + 3–4 sentences on what you had to un-learn from realist drama to make the absurd or menacing rhythm actually work."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Scene I Put on Its Feet","primary":"Choose one prescribed scene, direct and perform it with a small cast, record the full scene, and produce your own hand-drawn prompt-book (concept, blocking, entrances, cues).","support":"~400 words on one directorial choice and its effect.","llc":"Perform a 3-minute extract live and defend the choice under unscripted questioning."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Speaking the Villain","primary":"Memorise and record a major monologue (Barabas, Volpone, or Vittoria) with a fully marked actor's score — beats, turns, intentions in your hand.","support":"~400 words on your interpretation.","llc":"Deliver the speech live and take questions on why you played it as you did. AI can gloss the speech; it cannot memorise and embody it as you."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Two Theatres, One Stage","primary":"Build an original comparative production concept for one early/classical play plus one modern play (e.g. Everyman + Waiting for Godot) as a hand-made design dossier — set sketches, blocking, a directorial through-line.","support":"~400 words on the link you found.","llc":"Pitch and defend the concept to the cohort."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Pause as Meaning","primary":"A recorded performance of an Absurdist or Pinter extract in which you make silence do the dramatic work, accompanied by a timed pause-score you built.","support":"~400 words on how the pauses carry the menace or the void.","llc":"Perform live and defend the rhythm you chose."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Reviewing a Real Performance","primary":"Attend a REAL staged play — a named college production, theatre show or streamed full production (name it, date it, keep proof) — and write an original performance review from notes you take LIVE during the show, judging staging, not summarising plot.","support":"~400 words developing your critique.","llc":"Present your live notes and defend your judgement under challenge."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENC02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENC03 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take 2 pages of Swift's Gulliver's Travels (Lilliput, or the Houyhnhnms) in a real print copy. In your own hand, annotate every place the satire has TWO targets at once — the fictional and the real 18th-century one.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your annotated pages with your marginalia, hand in frame + 3–4 sentences on one passage you first read literally and then caught the satire underneath, and how you knew."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"In Defoe's Robinson Crusoe, track the account-book realism: mark every place Crusoe counts, inventories or dates something. Then in Sterne's Tristram Shandy, mark one digression and time how long it delays the story.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-marked passages + a small hand-drawn 'inventory vs digression' table + 3–4 sentences on how each technique manufactures a different kind of believability."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Take one scene where Emma Woodhouse (or Nick/Gatsby) misjudges another person's class or worth. Read it aloud performing the free indirect discourse — voice the narrator sliding into the character's biased mind and out again.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + a marked script showing exactly where narration becomes the character's own thought + 3–4 sentences on what the technique lets the novelist criticise without ever stating it outright."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Hand-draw the narrative frame of Wuthering Heights — Lockwood inside Nelly Dean inside the characters — as nested boxes with real page anchors. Do the same for Jane Eyre's retrospective 'Reader' address.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-drawn nested-narration diagram with actual page numbers + 3–4 sentences on what each frame hides, delays or distorts for the reader."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Take one stream-of-consciousness paragraph from Joyce's A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man (an epiphany, or the hell-sermon aftermath). Transcribe it by hand, then break it into the separate sense-impressions that make it up.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-transcription with the impressions numbered + a short method-log of how you learned to parse non-linear prose + 3–4 sentences on what you had to change in how you read to follow it."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Novel I Marked by Hand","primary":"Choose one prescribed novel and produce a hand-annotated close reading of 3 key passages in a real copy (your marginalia photographed) tracing ONE narrative technique across the whole book.","support":"~400 words on how that technique shapes the reader's knowledge.","llc":"Read one annotated passage aloud and defend your reading under unscripted questioning."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Mapping the Told Story","primary":"Build a hand-drawn structural map of one novel — real chronology versus narrated order, the narrators, the gaps — with page anchors throughout.","support":"~400 words on why the author scrambles or frames the telling.","llc":"Present the map and take a challenge on one gap you identified."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Voice on the Page","primary":"Record a dramatic reading of a 3–4 minute passage performing free indirect discourse or multiple voices, with a marked actor's script showing every shift.","support":"~400 words on the voicing choices.","llc":"Perform live and defend one interpretive choice. AI can summarise the passage; it cannot voice it as you."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Satire's Double Target","primary":"Produce an original annotated edition of 40 lines of Swift or Bunyan, resolving each satirical or allegorical target with a cited source, in your own hand.","support":"~400 words on how the two levels operate at once.","llc":"Walk the cohort through one decoded passage live, answering their questions."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"My Reading Journal, Defended","primary":"Keep a hand-written reading journal across one full novel — dated entries with page-specific observations that build toward an original argument, not a summary.","support":"~400 words synthesising the journal into a single thesis.","llc":"Present the argument and its page-anchored evidence to the cohort under unscripted Q&A."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENC03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENC04 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Read a 12-line catalogue passage from Whitman's 'Out of the Cradle Endlessly Rocking' aloud with the breath-units Whitman built in, marking where you had to breathe. Then read one tight Dickinson stanza aloud and mark her dashes as pauses.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading of both + a hand-marked copy showing breath and pause in Whitman versus Dickinson's dash-controlled halts + 3–4 sentences on how each poet's line physically controls YOUR body when you speak it."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Emerson's 'The American Scholar', Thoreau's 'Pond' chapter, and Amy Tan's 'Mother Tongue' each argue about authentic self-expression. In your hand, pull ONE claim from each and build a single integrated argument connecting them.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn concept-link sheet quoting one exact line from each with your connecting arrows + 3–4 sentences on where Tan complicates Emerson's confident 'self-reliant' self."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Perform Willy Loman's or Blanche DuBois's breakdown monologue (Death of a Salesman / A Streetcar Named Desire) to camera. Find the human being losing their grip on the American Dream — the denial, the collapse.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded performance + a marked script showing where you chose denial and where collapse + 3–4 sentences on what the scene made you feel toward a self-deceiving character, and why."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Take one passage from Morrison's Beloved or Chopin's The Awakening where a character reclaims a self that was owned or denied. Read it aloud slowly and hand-copy it, marking the exact words that carry the trauma or the awakening.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + the sound-marked hand copy + 3–4 sentences on one image in the passage and what it asks you, the reader, to care about."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Malcolm X taught himself to read by hand-copying the dictionary in prison. Reproduce his method: pick a difficult page of The Autobiography of Malcolm X, hand-copy it in full, and note every word you had to look up.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of your hand-copied page with your looked-up words listed beside it + 3–4 sentences on what copying-by-hand taught you about how self-education actually works, and what it cost in time."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"American Voices, Read Aloud","primary":"Choose 2 poems from Unit I, record full readings honouring each poet's prosody, with hand-marked scansion or breath-scores.","support":"~400 words on how sound builds each poet's meaning.","llc":"Recite live and defend one breath or metre choice under questioning."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Dream and Its Cost","primary":"Perform and film a major dramatic monologue (Loman or Blanche) with a marked actor's score.","support":"~400 words on the critique of the American Dream the scene delivers.","llc":"Perform an extract live and take unscripted Q&A on your interpretation."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Copying to Learn (Malcolm's Method)","primary":"A sustained hand-copied and glossed engagement with an autobiography extract (Malcolm X or Moraga), documenting your own self-education process page by page.","support":"~400 words on what the method revealed about literacy and power.","llc":"Show the pages and defend what the exercise taught you. AI cannot do the manual labour that is the point."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Passage I Sat With","primary":"An original hand-annotated close reading of one Morrison, Chopin or Melville passage, tracing a single image's meaning across the surrounding text.","support":"~400 words developing the reading.","llc":"Read the passage aloud and defend your reading under challenge."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Three Essays, One Argument","primary":"Build an original hand-made argument-map integrating Emerson, Thoreau and Tan on self and language, quoting exact lines and drawing the connections yourself.","support":"~400 words on the synthesis.","llc":"Present the argument and take an unscripted challenge to one link."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENC04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENC05 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take Sonnet 130 ('My mistress' eyes') or Sonnet 65. Hand-scan all 14 lines — mark every stressed syllable, the volta, the rhyme scheme — then read it aloud honouring the metre and the turn.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + the fully hand-scanned sonnet + 3–4 sentences on where the metre breaks the iambic pattern and what that stressed deviation does to the meaning of the line."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Perform the gulling/eavesdropping of Beatrice or Benedick (Much Ado About Nothing) or a scene from The Winter's Tale to camera with a partner. Apply the mechanics of the comic 'overhearing' convention — who hears, who is meant to hear, who is fooled.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded 2–3 minute scene + a hand-marked script showing who knows what at each moment + 3–4 sentences on how the dramatic irony makes the audience laugh AT the character."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Rehearse and deliver Othello's final speech ('Soft you; a word or two before you go') OR Iago's 'I am not what I am' to camera. Find the human being — the ruined dignity, or the working malice — under the role.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded performance + a marked-up actor's score + 3–4 sentences on one line where you chose an interpretation the text leaves open, and the reason you chose it."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Set a Boar's-Head-Tavern scene (Falstaff and Hal) beside a court or battlefield scene in Henry IV Part 1. In your hand, mark how the two worlds' LANGUAGE differs — tavern prose versus courtly verse — and where Hal moves between them.","deliverable_notes":"A two-column annotated sheet linking specific lines from each world + 3–4 sentences on what Hal's code-switching reveals about the prince he is deciding to become."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Take ONE moment in Othello (say, the handkerchief). Read what Bradley says about it and what Granville Barker (or John Russell Brown) says. In your own words, decide which critic's method you trust, then test it against the actual lines.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-written comparison of the two critics' claims measured against the primary text + 3–4 sentences on how having a critical LENS changed what you actually noticed in the scene."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Sonnet, Scanned and Spoken","primary":"Choose 2 sonnets and produce full hand-scansions plus recorded readings that honour the metre and the volta.","support":"~400 words on how the sound enacts the argument of each sonnet.","llc":"Recite live and defend one scansion choice under questioning."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Playing the Moor / the Villain","primary":"Memorise and film a major Othello, Iago or Falstaff speech with a fully marked actor's score.","support":"~400 words on your interpretation.","llc":"Perform live and take interpretive Q&A. AI can paraphrase the speech; it cannot embody YOUR reading of it."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Two Critics, One Scene","primary":"An original hand-written critical dossier testing Bradley against Granville Barker (or a modern critic) on one scene's actual lines, siding with neither until the text decides.","support":"~400 words arguing your position.","llc":"Argue your case and take an unscripted challenge."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Comedy on Its Feet","primary":"Direct and film a comic scene from Much Ado or The Winter's Tale with your own prompt-book blocking.","support":"~400 words on how staging creates the comedy.","llc":"Perform an extract and defend a directorial choice."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Prose and the Prince","primary":"An original hand-annotated study of verse-versus-prose across Henry IV Part 1, mapping where and why Shakespeare switches register.","support":"~400 words on what the pattern means.","llc":"Present the pattern and answer unscripted questions on one switch."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENC05'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENC06 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take ONE key concept — 'ecological imperialism', 'centre/margin', or 'hegemony'. Find a REAL, NAMED local instance you can photograph: a colonial-introduced plantation crop (rubber, tea, eucalyptus) on a named estate, or an English-dominant bilingual signboard in your town.","deliverable_notes":"Your own photograph of the named instance (name the place) + the concept's definition in your hand + 3–4 sentences applying the theory precisely to what you photographed, not to a textbook example."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Set Achebe's account of Igbo society in Things Fall Apart beside Conrad's Africa in Heart of Darkness. In your hand, mark one passage from each and show how Achebe writes BACK against Conrad's silences.","deliverable_notes":"A two-column annotated sheet linking the two passages + 3–4 sentences on what specifically Achebe restores that Conrad had erased or refused to see."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Take one passage from Selvon's The Lonely Londoners (the creolised narration) or Sidhwa's Ice Candy Man (Partition through a child's eyes). Read it aloud in the exact voice the text demands.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + a hand-marked copy noting where the dialect or child-voice carries the migrant or Partition experience + 3–4 sentences on what the voice makes you understand that plain reportage could not."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Apply a postcolonial close reading to Kolatkar's 'The Priest' / 'A Low Temple' or Awoonor's 'The Weaver Bird'. Hand-annotate the whole poem for every image where the sacred collides with the commercial or the colonial.","deliverable_notes":"Your annotated poem (marginalia in your hand) + 3–4 sentences on one image where the poem refuses to romanticise EITHER tradition or modernity."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Take Senghor's Negritude ('Night of Sine' or 'In Memoriam') or Grace Nichols's 'In My Name'. Read it aloud as an act of self-naming and dignity; hand-copy it, marking the sounds that reclaim the body or the heritage.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + the sound-marked hand copy + 3–4 sentences on what the poem insists we value, and to whom it is really speaking."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Concept in My Own District","primary":"Choose one key concept and document 2–3 REAL named local instances (photos, places named, dates) — colonial crops, English-dominant signboards, monuments, renamed places — then build an original applied analysis.","support":"~400 words connecting evidence to theory.","llc":"Show the photographs and defend the theory-to-evidence link under questioning. AI cannot photograph YOUR district."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Writing Back","primary":"An original hand-annotated comparative reading of a colonial text against its postcolonial answer (Conrad/Achebe, or Forster's A Passage to India against an Indian text).","support":"~400 words on the counter-narrative.","llc":"Present the linked passages and take an unscripted challenge."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Voice of the Migrant","primary":"A recorded dramatic reading of a creolised or dialect passage (Selvon, Nichols) with a marked score, plus your own note on the linguistic choices the writer makes.","support":"~400 words on how the language itself resists.","llc":"Perform live and defend the voicing."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Poem as Resistance","primary":"An original close-reading dossier (hand marginalia) of 2 postcolonial poems, tracing one resistance strategy across both.","support":"~400 words on the strategy.","llc":"Read the poems and defend your reading under challenge."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Negritude, Named and Spoken","primary":"Record and hand-analyse a Senghor, Awoonor or Nichols poem as an act of self-naming, connecting its images to a specific cultural heritage you research and cite.","support":"~400 words on the reclaiming.","llc":"Recite live and take Q&A on the cultural references you traced."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENC06'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENE01 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Naidu's 'Coromandel Fishers' and 'Palanquin Bearers' are built on the rhythm of real labour. Read one aloud, then find and record a REAL work-rhythm near you — a vendor's call, a rowing or lifting chant, a construction cadence (name the place) — and compare the beat.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded poem-reading + a short recording or hand-notated beat of the real work-rhythm (place named) + 3–4 sentences on how Naidu turns labour into metre, and where her romanticised version departs from the real work you heard."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Take Kamala Das's 'An Introduction' or 'The Looking Glass'. Read the confessional passage aloud in the first person; find the woman claiming her own voice against what her society expected.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + a hand-marked copy noting exactly where the poem breaks politeness or decorum + 3–4 sentences on what Das refuses that her society demanded of women."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Kalam's 'Orientation' (Wings of Fire), Radhakrishnan's 'Emerging World Society', and Aurobindo on poetry each argue about India's future self. Pull one claim from each and integrate them into a single argument in your hand.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn link-sheet quoting each source + 3–4 sentences on where the scientist (Kalam) and the philosopher (Radhakrishnan) agree, and where they part, on what progress should mean."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Karnad's Nagamandala turns oral folk convention into theatre — the Story becomes a character, the Flames speak. Stage a 2-minute extract preserving one folk device, OR block how you would present 'the Story' as a living character on stage.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded blocked extract + a hand-drawn staging of the folk device + 3–4 sentences on how the folk convention changes stage realism, compared to a named published folk tale you cite."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Anita Desai's Where Shall We Go This Summer? and Deshpande's Roots and Shadows voice a woman's inner refusal. Take one passage of the protagonist's interior monologue, read it aloud, and hand-copy it marking where duty and desire collide.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + the marked hand copy + 3–4 sentences on what the novel asks us to value in a woman's private discontent, and whether it judges her for it."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Labour Into Metre","primary":"Record a real local work-rhythm or work-song (place and people named, with consent) alongside your reading of Naidu, and build an original analysis of how Indian-English poetry aestheticises labour.","support":"~400 words on the gap between the metre and the real work.","llc":"Play both recordings and defend the comparison. AI cannot record YOUR street."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Woman's 'I'","primary":"A recorded reading plus a hand-annotated close reading of a Kamala Das, Desai or Deshpande passage, tracing the female first-person voice.","support":"~400 words on how the 'I' claims space.","llc":"Read live and take Q&A on the voice."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Folk Tale to Stage","primary":"Stage and film a Nagamandala extract preserving the folk device, with your own blocking and prompt notes, compared explicitly to a named published folk tale.","support":"~400 words on what the stage adds to the oral form.","llc":"Perform live and defend the adaptation."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Three Indian Minds on the Future","primary":"An original hand-made integration of Kalam, Radhakrishnan and Aurobindo's prose arguments, quoting each exactly and drawing the links yourself.","support":"~400 words on the synthesis.","llc":"Present it and take an unscripted challenge to one link."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Poem and Its Place","primary":"Take Toru Dutt's 'Our Casuarina Tree' or an Aurobindo poem; photograph the real tree or landscape it evokes near you (name the place) and hand-annotate how the poem transforms place into memory and symbol.","support":"~400 words on the transformation.","llc":"Show the photo and your reading, and defend the connection."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENE01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENE02 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Attend ONE real live performance — a named college play, temple drama, street play or dance-drama (name it, date it) — AND watch a recording of a staged play. Note what the LIVE event does that the recording cannot.","deliverable_notes":"Your live in-performance hand notes (named show, date) + 3–4 sentences comparing the liveness — audience, risk, presence, the possibility of failure — to the recorded version."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Find a REAL local or folk theatre form near you — Therukoothu, Villupattu, a temple Bhagavatha Mela, or a street-theatre troupe (name it). Observe or research it and place it against the course's map of world theatre traditions.","deliverable_notes":"Your photo or field-notes of the named local form + a hand-drawn placement of it against Greek / Epic / Absurd conventions + 3–4 sentences on which 'Western' category it fits and, more tellingly, where it refuses to fit."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Direct a 2–3 minute scene from any play with two actors. Make a real prompt-book: blocking, entrances, and your directorial concept stated in a single line.","deliverable_notes":"A recorded video of your directed scene + your hand-drawn blocking / prompt-book pages + 3–4 sentences on one directing choice (physical balance, focus, tempo) and the effect it produced."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Do ONE real acting-craft exercise: build a character through a single component — a mask you make, a make-up look, a changed voice, or one signature gesture. Transform yourself and record before and after.","deliverable_notes":"Before/after photos or video of your make-up, mask, voice or gesture transformation + 3–4 sentences on how the single physical change altered the character's inner life for you."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Design the LIGHTING or set for one scene. Using whatever you have — lamps, torches, coloured paper — light a real space to build a mood, and photograph three different lighting states of the same set.","deliverable_notes":"Three photos of your real lit space (same set, different light) + a hand-drawn lighting plan + 3–4 sentences on how light alone changed the scene's emotion, and whether you aimed at illusion or against it."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Scene I Directed","primary":"Direct and film a real scene with a cast, and produce a full hand prompt-book (concept, blocking, cues).","support":"~400 words on your directorial through-line.","llc":"Show an extract and defend a directorial choice under questioning."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Becoming Another (Acting Craft)","primary":"A filmed character transformation using make-up, mask, voice or gesture that you built yourself, with process photos.","support":"~400 words on how the craft component created the character.","llc":"Perform in character and take Q&A on the technique. AI cannot put on YOUR mask."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Lighting the Mood","primary":"A real lighting or design build for a chosen scene — multiple lit states photographed, with a hand-drawn plan.","support":"~400 words on the design logic.","llc":"Present the states and defend why each serves the scene."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Living Tradition Mapped","primary":"Field-document a real local theatre form (named, dated, with photos or a consented interview with a performer) and place it critically among world traditions.","support":"~400 words on where it fits and resists.","llc":"Show the documentation and take an unscripted challenge."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"From Page to Stage","primary":"Take one short scene and produce a complete production design dossier by hand (set sketch, costume, light, blocking), then stage a fragment on camera.","support":"~400 words on the production concept.","llc":"Pitch the production and defend its feasibility."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENE02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENE03 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Teach the SAME small item (e.g. the simple past tense, or one short English sentence) to a willing friend or sibling THREE ways — Grammar-Translation, the Direct Method, and an Audio-lingual drill. Notice which the learner responds to.","deliverable_notes":"A short recording or hand-log of all three micro-lessons (learner named, with consent) + 3–4 sentences on which method worked for THIS real learner, and why the history of methods matters in practice."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Pick ONE named approach — the Silent Way, Community Language Learning, or Suggestopedia. Run a real 10-minute micro-lesson using its actual techniques on a real learner.","deliverable_notes":"A recording or detailed hand-log of your micro-lesson (approach and learner named) + 3–4 sentences on the exact point where the approach's theory met the reality of a real classroom."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Design and run a real Task-Based lesson: give real learners a genuine task — order food, plan a trip, solve a problem — in English. Observe the language they actually produce, not the language you hoped for.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-written task plan + notes on the real language learners produced (learners and setting named) + 3–4 sentences on the gap between the language you targeted and what they actually used."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Teach one poem or one grammar point to a real learner yourself; THEN ask an AI chatbot or virtual tutor to teach the same thing. Integrate the two: where does the human teacher win, and where the machine?","deliverable_notes":"Your lesson notes + a screenshot or transcript of the chatbot's version + a hand-drawn comparison + 3–4 sentences on what the chatbot could not do that you could, and vice versa."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Evaluate ONE real ELT media or AI tool (a named app, BBC Learning English, or a chatbot tutor). Use it AS a learner for 20 minutes, then judge it against the course's criteria for good language teaching.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of your real usage + a hand-scored rubric you designed + 3–4 sentences on what the tool assumes about how people learn a language, and whether that assumption held true for YOU."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"One Item, Many Methods","primary":"Teach the same language item to a real learner across 3 historical methods, recorded, with your own comparative evaluation of what happened.","support":"~400 words on why one method fit this learner.","llc":"Show clips and defend your verdict. AI cannot teach YOUR real learner."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Lesson I Actually Taught","primary":"Plan, deliver (to real, named, consented learners) and film a full task-based or communicative lesson, with your hand-written plan and honest post-lesson observation.","support":"~400 words on what worked and what you would change.","llc":"Present the lesson and take unscripted Q&A on your choices."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Human Teacher vs Machine Tutor","primary":"An original head-to-head study — you teach a point, an AI tutor teaches the same point, both on real learners — and you measure and compare the outcomes.","support":"~400 words on where each wins.","llc":"Present the evidence and defend your verdict under challenge."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"My Own Teaching Materials","primary":"Build a real, usable set of teaching materials (worksheet, flashcards, task cards) for a specific real learner group and trial them at least once.","support":"~400 words on the pedagogy behind each design choice.","llc":"Demonstrate the materials and defend the design."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Auditing an AI Tutor","primary":"A rigorous hands-on evaluation of a named AI or media ELT tool tested on real learners, scored against a rubric you build yourself.","support":"~400 words on the tool's hidden assumptions.","llc":"Present findings and take an unscripted challenge to your criteria."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENE03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENE04 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Neruda's 'Ode to the Onion' celebrates an ordinary thing. Hold the REAL object — an actual onion, or another everyday object — read the ode aloud to it, and mark every line where Neruda's image matches or exceeds the thing in your hand.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of you with the real object + your recorded reading + a hand-marked copy where image meets object + 3–4 sentences on what the ode made you SEE in a thing you had always ignored."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Gordimer, Coetzee and Morrison each write power and race. Pull one passage from each (Loot, the Disgrace excerpt, the Sula excerpt) and, in your hand, build a single argument about how the powerless are seen.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-drawn link-sheet quoting all three passages + 3–4 sentences on whose gaze each text is written from, and what that vantage changes about what we are allowed to see."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Perform Davies or Aston from Pinter's The Caretaker (or a speech from Shaw's Man and Superman) to camera. Find the human being under the menace or the comedy — the need, the fear, the self-justification.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded performance + a marked actor's score showing where you placed the Pinter pauses + 3–4 sentences on what the silences reveal that the words themselves hide."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Alice Munro's stories move through time without warning. Take one Munro story ('The Turkey Season', 'Differently' or 'Runaway') and hand-map its actual chronology against the order she tells it, with page anchors.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-drawn timeline (told-order versus real-order) with page numbers + a short method-log of how you tracked the shifts + 3–4 sentences on what the non-linear telling achieves that a straight chronology could not."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Take Steinbeck's The Pearl (the family, the pearl, the loss) or Marquez's One Hundred Years of Solitude. Read aloud one passage where a poor family faces greed or fate, and hand-copy it marking the images of value and of loss.","deliverable_notes":"Your recorded reading + the marked hand copy + 3–4 sentences on what the parable asks us to value, and what it warns us costs too much to keep."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Ode and the Real Thing","primary":"Take Neruda's ode form, hold and photograph a real everyday object, read the ode aloud to it, and produce an original close reading of image-versus-object.","support":"~400 words on how the ode transforms the ordinary.","llc":"Bring the object, read, and defend the reading. AI cannot hold YOUR onion."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Three Nobel Voices on Power","primary":"An original hand-made integration of Gordimer, Coetzee and Morrison passages on power and the gaze.","support":"~400 words on the synthesis.","llc":"Present it and take an unscripted challenge to one link."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Pinter's Pauses, Performed","primary":"Memorise and film a Caretaker or Shaw extract honouring the silences, with a timed pause-score.","support":"~400 words on how the silence carries meaning.","llc":"Perform live and defend the rhythm you chose. AI can gloss the play; it cannot time YOUR pauses."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Time, Unfolded (Munro)","primary":"A full hand-drawn structural map of a Munro story's chronology versus its telling, with an original argument about why she scrambles time.","support":"~400 words on the effect.","llc":"Present the map and take a challenge on one time-shift."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Parable I Read Closely","primary":"An original hand-annotated close reading of a Steinbeck or Marquez passage, tracing one symbol (the pearl, the ice, the gold) across the work.","support":"~400 words developing the reading.","llc":"Read aloud and defend the symbol's arc under questioning."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENE04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PENED1 · m-a-english ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Take ten real sentences from a named newspaper and label the grammatical units, sentence pattern, and any clauses/phrases in each by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The ten source sentences (paper + date named) + your hand annotation + 1 line on the pattern you found most often."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Find or write ten sentences each containing one real error of the type competitive exams test (subject–verb, tense, preposition); correct each and name the rule.","deliverable_notes":"Your ten error sentences + corrections + the rule named for each + 1 line on the error you personally make most."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Take one real passage (named source), answer three comprehension questions you write, and list ten new vocabulary words with meanings you looked up.","deliverable_notes":"The passage source + your questions and answers + your ten-word glossary + 1 line on one word you will now use."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Write a precis of a real ~300-word article (named) to one-third length by hand, then draft one formal letter and one short report on a real situation you name.","deliverable_notes":"The precis with word counts shown + the letter + the report + 1 line on what you had to cut and why."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Collect ten current-affairs / general-knowledge facts from this week's real news (dated, sourced) and turn them into ten exam-style questions with answers.","deliverable_notes":"Your ten dated facts with sources + the ten Q&A you wrote + 1 line on the topic area you are weakest in."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"My Own Competitive-Exam Question Bank","primary":"Build, by hand, a genuine practice set across all five areas (grammar, error-spotting, comprehension, precis/writing, GK) drawn from REAL current sources you name and date — with an answer key you can defend.","support":"~400 words on which area you improved most and how you know.","llc":"Administer three of your own questions to the cohort and justify the answers when challenged."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Errors I Learned to Catch","primary":"Assemble a real corpus of 30+ error sentences (from actual writing you find — signage, notices, drafts, with sources named), correct each, and classify the errors into a taxonomy you build.","support":"~400 words on the two or three error types that dominate.","llc":"Correct a fresh sentence live and name the rule."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Precis: Saying It in a Third","primary":"Precis three real full-length articles (named) to strict length by hand, preserving meaning, and document the decisions that let you cut without distorting.","support":"~400 words on the discipline of concision.","llc":"Precis a short fresh passage live under a word limit."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Week of Current Affairs, Turned Into Practice","primary":"Track one week of real, dated news and convert it into a structured GK/current-affairs practice set with sources, then test yourself and log your score honestly.","support":"~400 words on building a sustainable current-affairs habit.","llc":"Pose three of your questions and give sourced answers when the audience pushes back."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Formal Writing for a Real Purpose","primary":"Write a real formal letter and a real report addressing an actual situation you name (a request, a complaint, an event report) to competitive-exam standard, and — if appropriate and consented — actually use one.","support":"~400 words on register and structure.","llc":"Read your letter and defend one wording choice against a suggested alternative."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PENED1'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAC01 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Apply the counting principle (class equation / Sylow counting) to a concrete finite group you pick (e.g. a group of order 12, 20, or a dihedral group) — enumerate its conjugacy classes by hand.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-worked class equation for the named group + the count of Sylow subgroups, on paper. One line stating what the counting forced about the group's structure."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Take one specific group and decide by hand whether it is solvable — build its derived series or a composition series explicitly.","deliverable_notes":"The full derived/composition series written out by hand for the named group + one line on whether it is solvable and why."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Construct an explicit linear transformation on a small vector space and compute its matrix in two different bases; verify the change-of-basis relation by hand.","deliverable_notes":"Two matrices for the same map in two named bases + the change-of-basis computation, all by hand. One line on what is invariant."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute the Jordan canonical form of a specific 4×4 (or 5×5) matrix you choose that is NOT diagonalisable — find generalized eigenvectors by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The full Jordan form + generalized-eigenvector chains worked on paper for the named matrix. Show the invariant factors."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For a chosen matrix, verify trace = sum of eigenvalues and the transpose/adjoint identities by direct computation, then explain why they are basis-independent.","deliverable_notes":"The direct computation + a two-line argument for basis-independence, by hand."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Group I Classified by Hand","primary":"Choose ONE finite group of order ≤ 60 and completely determine its structure yourself — conjugacy classes, Sylow subgroups, normal subgroups, whether it is solvable — using only hand computation, no software output as the answer.","support":"~400 words reflect on where the counting theorems did the decisive work and which step you found hardest to be certain of.","llc":"Present your classification at the board; a faculty member changes the order (e.g. asks 'what about order 2p?') and you reason live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Jordan Form Nobody Handed Me","primary":"Pick a non-diagonalisable matrix (construct one so its Jordan structure is non-obvious) and derive its Jordan form entirely by hand, including the generalized eigenvector chains and invariant factors.","support":"~400 words explain what the Jordan blocks reveal that eigenvalues alone do not.","llc":"Reconstruct the derivation live at the board while an examiner perturbs one entry and asks how the form changes."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Solvable or Not — I Proved It","primary":"Take one group whose solvability is not obvious and give a complete hand proof either way (derived series or a composition series with its factors).","support":"~400 words reflect on the link between solvability and the historical solvability-by-radicals question.","llc":"Defend each step of your series live; faculty may swap the group and ask you to adapt."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Change of Basis, Change of View","primary":"Choose one linear operator and study every basis-independent quantity (trace, determinant, characteristic and minimal polynomial) by explicit computation in ≥3 bases.","support":"~400 words argue why these are the 'real' invariants of the operator.","llc":"Show your computations and answer live why a chosen quantity is or isn't basis-independent."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Theorem I Can Defend","primary":"Select ONE structural theorem from the course (Cayley–Hamilton, a Sylow theorem, the primary decomposition) and produce your OWN complete proof plus a worked example you constructed.","support":"~400 words reflect on the one step where your understanding was tested most.","llc":"Present the proof at the board and answer an examiner's 'why is this step valid?' unscripted."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAC01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAC02 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Construct an explicit function that IS of bounded variation and one that is NOT, on the same interval, and compute/estimate the total variation by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The two named functions + your total-variation computation/argument on paper. One line distinguishing them."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute a specific Riemann–Stieltjes integral ∫f dα by hand for a chosen integrator α with a jump, showing how the jump contributes.","deliverable_notes":"The full hand computation for the named f, α + one line on what the jump added."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Prove (or verify on an example you build) an integration-by-parts or a change-of-variable result for the Riemann–Stieltjes integral.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand proof/verification on a concrete example."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Exhibit a sequence where the R–S integral and a limit do NOT interchange, and one where they do — decide by hand which hypothesis fails.","deliverable_notes":"The two named sequences + which convergence hypothesis distinguishes them, by hand."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Construct a sequence of functions converging pointwise but NOT uniformly, and demonstrate the failure by exhibiting the ε that breaks uniformity.","deliverable_notes":"The named sequence + the explicit ε and n-dependence showing non-uniform convergence, by hand."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Counter-Example I Built","primary":"Construct an original counter-example to a plausible-but-false analysis statement (e.g. 'pointwise limit of continuous is continuous') — the function must be YOUR construction, drawn and justified by hand.","support":"~400 words reflect on why the naive statement fails and what extra hypothesis rescues it.","llc":"Present your counter-example at the board; faculty ask 'what if we add uniform convergence?' and you answer live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Riemann–Stieltjes Integral, Fully by Hand","primary":"Choose a non-trivial integrator with both a continuous and a jump part and evaluate ∫f dα completely by hand for a chosen f.","support":"~400 words explain how the Stieltjes integral unifies sums and integrals.","llc":"Reconstruct the computation live while an examiner changes the jump location."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Bounded Variation on a Real Signal","primary":"Take a REAL discretised signal you sourced (a day of temperature/price/step-count readings), treat it as a function, and compute its total variation; relate BV to the signal's roughness.","support":"~400 words reflect on what total variation measures about your real data.","llc":"Show your data and computation; answer how variation changes if the signal is smoothed."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"When the Limit Does Not Commute","primary":"Find and prove one setting where limit and integral fail to interchange, building the example yourself.","support":"~400 words reflect on the theorem (dominated/monotone/uniform) that would have saved you.","llc":"Defend the failure live; faculty add a hypothesis and ask if it now holds."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Proof of Uniform Convergence I Own","primary":"Select one sequence and give a complete ε–N proof of uniform (or non-uniform) convergence that you wrote.","support":"~400 words reflect on the difference pointwise-vs-uniform made concretely.","llc":"Present the ε–N argument at the board and adapt it live to a modified sequence."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAC02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAC03 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Solve a specific higher-order linear ODE with constant coefficients by hand (characteristic roots incl. repeated/complex) and verify the solution by substitution.","deliverable_notes":"The full hand solution + the substitution check for the named ODE."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Use variation of parameters / undetermined coefficients on a chosen non-homogeneous ODE and interpret the particular solution physically.","deliverable_notes":"The hand solution + one line on what the forcing term represents."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Solve a linear ODE with variable coefficients (Cauchy–Euler or a power-series method) by hand around a chosen point.","deliverable_notes":"The worked series/solution on paper + radius/interval of validity."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"For a chosen variable-coefficient ODE, locate ordinary vs singular points and classify a regular singular point by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The classified points + the indicial equation you derived, on paper."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Apply the Picard iteration to an IVP you choose and produce the first 3 iterates by hand; discuss existence/uniqueness via Lipschitz.","deliverable_notes":"Your 3 Picard iterates + the Lipschitz check for the named IVP."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"An ODE Model of a Real System","primary":"Pick a REAL first/second-order system you can observe (an RC circuit you build, a cooling cup you time, a loaded spring) and derive + solve its ODE, then compare the solution to your own measurements.","support":"~400 words reflect on where the model matched your data and where it broke.","llc":"Show your measurements and solution; faculty change a parameter and ask you to predict the new behaviour live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Existence, Uniqueness, and Where They Fail","primary":"Construct one IVP satisfying Picard's hypotheses and one violating them (non-unique solutions), proving each claim by hand.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the Lipschitz condition guarantees geometrically.","llc":"Defend both examples at the board under an examiner's questions."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Series Solution I Derived","primary":"Take one variable-coefficient ODE and derive its power-series (or Frobenius) solution fully by hand, computing several coefficients and the recurrence.","support":"~400 words reflect on why the singular point forced the method.","llc":"Reconstruct the recurrence live while faculty change the singular point."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Variation of Parameters, Owned","primary":"Solve one non-homogeneous ODE by variation of parameters entirely by hand and verify against an independent method.","support":"~400 words reflect on why two methods agree.","llc":"Present both solutions and answer why they must coincide."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Phase Portrait I Drew by Hand","primary":"For a chosen 2×2 linear system, classify the equilibrium and hand-draw the phase portrait, then confirm with a few computed trajectories.","support":"~400 words reflect on how eigenvalues determined the portrait.","llc":"Present the portrait; faculty change an eigenvalue sign and you redraw live."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAC03'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAC04 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Construct a specific field extension and compute its degree and a basis over the base field, by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The named extension, its degree, and an explicit basis, on paper."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Find all roots of a chosen polynomial in a splitting field you construct; identify the splitting field explicitly.","deliverable_notes":"The splitting field + the roots, worked by hand."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Compute the Galois group of a specific polynomial by hand — list the automorphisms and their action on roots.","deliverable_notes":"The Galois group as a permutation group + the automorphism table, on paper."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Construct the finite field GF(p^n) for chosen small p,n and build its addition/multiplication structure via an irreducible polynomial.","deliverable_notes":"The irreducible polynomial + a sample of the field's arithmetic, by hand."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For a chosen polynomial, decide solvability by radicals from its Galois group and justify by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The solvability verdict + the group-theoretic reason, on paper."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Galois Group I Computed","primary":"Choose one polynomial (degree 3–5) whose Galois group is not obvious and determine it completely by hand — automorphisms, fixed fields, the subgroup–subfield correspondence.","support":"~400 words reflect on how the correspondence organised the whole picture.","llc":"Present the correspondence at the board; faculty pick a subgroup and ask for its fixed field live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Why the Quintic Resists","primary":"Take one specific quintic and prove (via its Galois group) whether it is solvable by radicals, constructing the argument yourself.","support":"~400 words reflect on what solvability of the group has to do with radicals.","llc":"Defend the verdict live under an examiner changing the polynomial."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Finite Field I Built","primary":"Construct GF(p^n) explicitly for a chosen p,n via an irreducible polynomial you verify, and build a working arithmetic (a small hand or coded calculator).","support":"~400 words reflect on why every finite field has prime-power order.","llc":"Demonstrate arithmetic live; faculty ask you to compute a chosen product/inverse."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Constructibility, Decided by Me","primary":"Take one classical constructibility question (a chosen angle trisection / regular n-gon) and settle it by hand using field degrees.","support":"~400 words reflect on how a geometry question became an algebra question.","llc":"Present the degree argument and answer why the tower's degree decides it."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Theorem of Galois Theory, Proven","primary":"Select one theorem (the fundamental theorem, the primitive element theorem) and give your own complete proof with a constructed example.","support":"~400 words reflect on the step that most tested you.","llc":"Present the proof and field questions on each step."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAC04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAC05 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute the Fourier series of a chosen periodic function by hand and plot several partial sums to observe convergence / Gibbs.","deliverable_notes":"The coefficients + a hand or coded plot of partial sums for the named function. One line on Gibbs."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Apply a Fourier integral / transform to a chosen function and interpret the frequency content.","deliverable_notes":"The transform worked by hand + one line reading the frequency content."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"For a chosen f:R²→R, compute the total derivative (Jacobian) at a point and verify differentiability from the definition.","deliverable_notes":"The Jacobian + the definition-based differentiability check, by hand."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Find and classify the critical points of a chosen multivariable function using the Hessian, by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The critical points + Hessian classification for the named function."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Apply the Implicit Function Theorem to a chosen equation to solve for one variable locally; verify the derivative formula.","deliverable_notes":"The local solution + the verified implicit-derivative, by hand."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Fourier Analysis of a Real Signal","primary":"Record a REAL periodic signal (a musical note, a rotating fan's audio, a tide/price cycle), compute its Fourier coefficients, and identify its dominant frequencies against what you expected.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the spectrum revealed about your real signal.","llc":"Play the signal and show its spectrum; faculty ask what changes if you double the period."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Gibbs, Seen and Explained","primary":"Take one discontinuous periodic function, compute its Fourier partial sums, and demonstrate the Gibbs overshoot quantitatively.","support":"~400 words reflect on why more terms don't remove the overshoot.","llc":"Present partial-sum plots and answer why the overshoot persists."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Multivariable Extremum I Found","primary":"Choose a real optimisation (a shape/area/cost with two variables) and find + classify its extrema by hand using the Hessian, then verify numerically.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the second-derivative test decided.","llc":"Present the classification; faculty change a coefficient and ask if the nature of the critical point changes."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Implicit Function Theorem, Applied","primary":"Take one implicitly-defined curve/surface from a real constraint and use the IFT to solve and differentiate locally, verifying against a direct computation.","support":"~400 words reflect on where the theorem's hypothesis (non-vanishing Jacobian) mattered.","llc":"Defend the local solution and its derivative live."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Convergence Proof I Own","primary":"Select one Fourier convergence theorem (pointwise/Dirichlet or uniform) and give your own proof outline with a constructed example.","support":"~400 words reflect on the hypothesis doing the work.","llc":"Present and answer questions on each hypothesis."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAC05'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAC06 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Classify a chosen 2nd-order PDE (elliptic/parabolic/hyperbolic) by hand and state which physical model it fits.","deliverable_notes":"The classification computation + the matching physical model, on paper."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Solve a chosen Cauchy problem (e.g. wave equation via d'Alembert) by hand and interpret the solution's propagation.","deliverable_notes":"The hand solution + one line on how information propagates."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Apply separation of variables to a chosen boundary-value PDE (heat/Laplace) and build the eigenfunction series by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The separated solution + the series, on paper, for the named BVP."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Set up and solve a chosen boundary value problem, checking the boundary conditions are met by your series.","deliverable_notes":"The BVP solution + the boundary-condition verification, by hand."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Construct the Green's function for a chosen simple operator/domain and verify its defining property.","deliverable_notes":"The Green's function + the verification of its delta-source property, by hand."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A PDE Model of Something Real","primary":"Pick a REAL diffusion/wave phenomenon you can observe (heat along a rod you warm, ripples in a tray, a cooling wall), set up its PDE, solve by separation of variables, and compare to your own observations.","support":"~400 words reflect on where the idealised PDE matched reality and where it didn't.","llc":"Show your setup and observations; faculty change a boundary condition and ask you to predict the effect."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Classification Decides the Physics","primary":"Take three PDEs, classify each by hand, and argue how the type (elliptic/parabolic/hyperbolic) dictates well-posed data.","support":"~400 words reflect on why hyperbolic problems propagate and elliptic ones smooth.","llc":"Defend a classification live and answer what data each type needs."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Green's Function I Constructed","primary":"Construct the Green's function for one operator/domain yourself and use it to solve an inhomogeneous problem, verifying against separation of variables.","support":"~400 words reflect on the Green's function as an inverse operator.","llc":"Present both solutions and answer why they agree."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Separation of Variables, Owned","primary":"Solve one BVP fully by hand via separation of variables, deriving the eigenvalues/eigenfunctions and proving the series meets the conditions.","support":"~400 words reflect on the role of the Sturm–Liouville structure.","llc":"Reconstruct the eigenvalue derivation live under a changed boundary condition."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Cauchy Problem and Its Domain of Dependence","primary":"Solve one hyperbolic Cauchy problem and hand-draw the characteristic cone / domain of dependence for a chosen point.","support":"~400 words reflect on why signals travel at finite speed here.","llc":"Present the characteristics; faculty move the point and ask for the new domain."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
