      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Use an advanced OOP feature (inheritance/operator overloading/templates) to extend your class, and demo the polymorphic behaviour.","deliverable_notes":"The extended code running + a demo showing the OOP feature's effect."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Implement one root-finding or error-analysis method and run it on a real function, reporting the error at each iteration.","deliverable_notes":"Your code + an iteration/error table on a named function."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Solve a real linear system (from a small real problem you set up) by a numerical method you coded (Gauss/LU/iterative) and check the residual.","deliverable_notes":"Running code + the solution + the residual on your real system."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Numerically solve an ODE (Euler/RK4) you coded for a real initial-value situation and compare to an analytic or measured solution.","deliverable_notes":"Your code + a table/plot comparing numerical vs true/measured, for the named IVP."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Numerical Solver I Built on Real Data","primary":"Choose a REAL computational problem (fit a real dataset, solve a real system, integrate a measured signal), implement the numerical method in C++ yourself, and validate the result against ground truth you have.","support":"~400 words reflect on where floating-point error or conditioning bit.","llc":"Live-demo your solver; faculty change the input data and you show the new result and its error."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Error Analysis I Can Defend","primary":"Implement two methods for the same quantity, run both on real inputs, and produce a documented error/convergence comparison you can justify.","support":"~400 words reflect on which method you'd trust for a real decision and why.","llc":"Present both and answer why they differ under a faculty-chosen input."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"An OOP Design That Earned Its Structure","primary":"Design a C++ class hierarchy for a real small domain, implement it, and demonstrate that the OOP structure (not just procedural code) paid off.","support":"~400 words reflect on what inheritance/polymorphism bought you.","llc":"Live-demo and answer 'why a class here?' with a modification request."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Conditioning, Seen","primary":"Take one ill-conditioned real problem, show numerically how small input changes blow up the output, and quantify the conditioning.","support":"~400 words reflect on what conditioning means for trusting a computed answer.","llc":"Demo the blow-up live under a faculty perturbation."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"From Analytic to Numerical","primary":"Take one ODE/integral, solve it both analytically and numerically in your code, and reconcile the two on a real case.","support":"~400 words reflect on when numerical is the only option.","llc":"Present both and defend the numerical error bound."}
