      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Model a small computation (a real process/rule) as a finite-state machine and trace it on real inputs.","deliverable_notes":"The FSM + traces on real named inputs."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Counting Problem from My Life","primary":"Take a REAL counting question you genuinely have (arrangements at an event, password possibilities, seating, tournament fixtures) and solve it rigorously two ways, then verify against an actual enumeration where feasible.","support":"~400 words reflect on which method you trust and why the two agree.","llc":"Present the count; faculty change one constraint and you recount live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Proof I Constructed","primary":"Choose one discrete statement (an inductive identity, a pigeonhole application, a divisibility) and give your own complete proof with the base/step explicit.","support":"~400 words reflect on the step that most tested your rigour.","llc":"Present the induction; faculty change the claim slightly and you adapt the proof."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Recurrence Behind a Real Pattern","primary":"Find a real growing pattern (a tiling, a population, a data structure's cost), model it as a recurrence, and solve it, checking against real counts.","support":"~400 words reflect on why the closed form matters.","llc":"Present the solved recurrence and predict the next term under a faculty tweak."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Logic Simplified, Circuit Owned","primary":"Take a real logical condition (a form's validation rule, a small circuit) and minimise it, proving equivalence, then implement/verify.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the minimisation saved.","llc":"Demo the equivalence on faculty-chosen inputs."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Machine for a Real Rule","primary":"Model a real acceptance rule as an FSM, implement a checker, and demo it accepting/rejecting real strings.","support":"~400 words reflect on the states you needed and why.","llc":"Live-run on faculty inputs and explain a rejection."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAE08'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAE09 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute a path/line integral around a chosen closed curve by hand and relate its value to what the curve encloses.","deliverable_notes":"The computed integral + one line on how enclosure affected it, for the named curve."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"For a chosen space (annulus, torus surface, figure-eight), reason out its holes and connect to homology/cohomology intuitively, by hand.","deliverable_notes":"A hand sketch + the count/description of independent holes for the named space."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Relate an integral's path-independence to the space's holes for a chosen 1-form and region.","deliverable_notes":"The path-independence test + its topological explanation, by hand."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Construct a covering space of a chosen space (circle by line/n-fold) and describe the covering map explicitly.","deliverable_notes":"The covering-space diagram + the map description, for the named space."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute the fundamental group of a chosen space by hand (loops, or via van Kampen on a decomposition you make).","deliverable_notes":"The fundamental group + the loop/van-Kampen argument, on paper."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Holes I Can Point To","primary":"Take a REAL object with non-trivial topology you can hold or photograph (a mug, a pretzel, a bangle, a knotted rope, a road network) and determine its topological invariants (genus / fundamental group / hole count) with a hand argument you construct.","support":"~400 words reflect on how the invariant is unchanged by bending but changed by cutting.","llc":"Present the real object; faculty deform or cut it (in words) and you state how the invariant changes live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Fundamental Group I Computed","primary":"Choose one space and compute its fundamental group by hand (directly or via van Kampen on your decomposition), constructing the argument yourself.","support":"~400 words reflect on how loops encode holes.","llc":"Reconstruct the van-Kampen step live under a changed decomposition."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Path-Independence and Its Holes","primary":"Take one region with a hole and demonstrate, by computing integrals, how the hole breaks path-independence, tying it to the first cohomology.","support":"~400 words reflect on the exact-vs-closed distinction concretely.","llc":"Present the two paths and defend why they differ."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Covering Space I Built","primary":"Construct a covering space of a chosen space explicitly, describe the deck transformations, and relate them to the fundamental group.","support":"~400 words reflect on the covering–subgroup correspondence.","llc":"Present the covering; faculty pick a subgroup and you describe its cover."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Topological Theorem I Own","primary":"Select one result (Brouwer in low dimension, the fundamental theorem of algebra via topology, a van Kampen application) and give your own proof/derivation with an example.","support":"~400 words reflect on the tested step.","llc":"Present and field questions live."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAE09'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAE10 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Model a REAL random phenomenon you can sample (dice/coin/queue/waiting time you actually record) with a probability distribution and check the fit.","deliverable_notes":"Your real recorded data + the fitted distribution + a fit check."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Compute moments / MGF for a chosen distribution by hand and verify the mean/variance against your real sample.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-computed moments + the comparison to your sample statistics."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"For two real variables you collect, estimate their joint/conditional behaviour and covariance by hand.","deliverable_notes":"Your real bivariate data + the computed covariance/conditional table."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Derive the distribution of a transformation/sum of your chosen variables and check against simulation you run.","deliverable_notes":"The derived distribution + a simulation histogram matching it."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Demonstrate a limit theorem (LLN/CLT) empirically by sampling real/simulated data and show convergence.","deliverable_notes":"Your sampling experiment + the convergence plot + one line on the theorem."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Distribution I Fitted to Real Data","primary":"Collect a REAL dataset yourself (waiting times, rainfall, scores, a process you can measure), fit a probability model, estimate its parameters, and TEST the fit (goodness-of-fit), reporting whether the model holds.","support":"~400 words reflect on where the model failed the tails or the fit.","llc":"Present your data and fit; faculty ask 'what if the sample were larger / from another source?' and you reason live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The CLT, Demonstrated","primary":"Run your own sampling experiment (real or simulated from a real process) to demonstrate the Central Limit Theorem, quantifying the convergence rate.","support":"~400 words reflect on when the CLT approximation is safe.","llc":"Present the convergence and answer why a skewed source needs a larger n."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"An Estimator I Built and Judged","primary":"Derive an estimator (MLE or method of moments) for a real parameter, compute it on your data, and assess its bias/variance.","support":"~400 words reflect on the bias–variance trade-off you saw.","llc":"Defend the estimator; faculty perturb the data and you show its sensitivity."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Hypothesis I Tested on Real Data","primary":"Form a real question about data you collected, run an appropriate test, and state the decision with its error risks honestly.","support":"~400 words reflect on the p-value's real meaning and what you did NOT conclude.","llc":"Present the test; faculty change the significance level and you re-decide."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Joint Behaviour of Two Real Variables","primary":"Collect two related real variables, study their dependence (covariance/correlation/conditional), and derive one transformation's distribution.","support":"~400 words reflect on correlation vs causation in your case.","llc":"Present and answer whether an intervention would change the relationship."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAE10'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAE11 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Load a REAL dataset YOU sourced into R and clean it (types, missing values), documenting each step in a script.","deliverable_notes":"Your R script + the raw-vs-clean row/column counts on your real dataset."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Produce three meaningful visualisations of your real data in R that reveal something you didn't know before.","deliverable_notes":"The three plots + one line each on what they revealed. Your name in the script header."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute the full descriptive statistics of your real data in R and interpret the shape (skew, outliers).","deliverable_notes":"The descriptive output + your reading of the distribution's shape."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Run an appropriate hypothesis test in R on a real question about your data and interpret the result honestly.","deliverable_notes":"The test output + your decision + what you did NOT conclude."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Fit a predictive model (regression/classification) in R on your real data and evaluate it on held-out data.","deliverable_notes":"The model + its held-out performance metric + one line on whether it's usable."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"An Analysis of My Own Real Dataset","primary":"Source a REAL dataset that matters to you (your college's records with consent, a public dataset about your town, data you logged), and carry it end-to-end in R — clean, explore, test a real hypothesis, and build+validate a predictive model — producing one honest, actionable finding.","support":"~400 words reflect on the finding's limits and what data you wish you had.","llc":"Live-run key R chunks; faculty change a filter or add a variable and you rerun and re-interpret on the spot."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Visualisation That Told the Truth","primary":"Take a real dataset and build a visualisation that corrects a wrong intuition people have about it, defending every design choice.","support":"~400 words reflect on how a different chart could have misled.","llc":"Present the chart; faculty ask you to re-plot to test robustness live."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Predictive Model I Can Trust","primary":"Build a predictive model on your real data, evaluate it honestly (cross-validation, held-out), and state clearly where it should NOT be used.","support":"~400 words reflect on overfitting and the metric you chose.","llc":"Live-predict on a faculty-held sample and explain the error."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"A Hypothesis Test, Honestly Reported","primary":"Form a genuine question about real data, run the test in R, and report it without over-claiming (effect size + uncertainty, not just p).","support":"~400 words reflect on the multiple-comparisons or power issues you faced.","llc":"Defend the conclusion; faculty change the data subset and you re-test."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Reproducible from Raw to Result","primary":"Make your entire real analysis reproducible (script + data + documented steps) so a peer can rerun it and get your numbers.","support":"~400 words reflect on what made reproducibility hard.","llc":"Have a peer or faculty rerun one step live and match your output."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAE11'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAE12 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Compute tensor components in one coordinate system and transform them to another by hand, verifying the transformation law.","deliverable_notes":"The components in two named coordinate systems + the transformation check, on paper."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute Christoffel symbols / a covariant derivative for a chosen metric (polar/spherical) by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The Christoffel symbols for the named metric, worked on paper."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Compute a curvature quantity (Riemann/Ricci component) for a chosen simple metric by hand and interpret it.","deliverable_notes":"The curvature component + one line on what non-zero curvature means for the surface."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Apply a Lorentz transformation to chosen events and compute the invariant interval; verify it is preserved.","deliverable_notes":"The transformed coordinates + the invariant interval before/after, on paper."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Work a relativistic dynamics problem (time dilation / momentum-energy) for chosen real-ish parameters by hand.","deliverable_notes":"The computed dilation/energy + one line on the physical scale, on paper."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Curvature I Computed by Hand","primary":"Choose one surface/metric (a sphere of a real radius, a cone, a saddle) and compute its curvature by hand, then relate the number to the geometry you can see or feel on a real curved object.","support":"~400 words reflect on how intrinsic curvature differs from how it sits in space.","llc":"Hold or draw the real curved object and defend why your curvature sign matches its shape live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Invariant Interval, Preserved","primary":"Take chosen events, apply Lorentz transformations, and demonstrate by hand that the spacetime interval is invariant while space and time separately are not.","support":"~400 words reflect on what 'the same for all observers' really means.","llc":"Reconstruct the invariance live under a faculty-chosen boost."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Tensors That Transform Correctly","primary":"Construct a real geometric quantity (a stress/strain-like array or a metric) and verify it transforms as a tensor across coordinates, catching a non-tensor impostor you also build.","support":"~400 words reflect on why the transformation law is the definition.","llc":"Present both and defend which is a genuine tensor."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Christoffel Symbols, Owned","primary":"Compute the Christoffel symbols and a geodesic for a chosen metric by hand, and interpret the geodesic as the straightest path.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the connection encodes.","llc":"Present the geodesic; faculty change the metric and you predict the geodesic's change."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Relativity Result I Derived","primary":"Derive one relativistic result (time dilation, E=mc² route, relativistic momentum) yourself and check the non-relativistic limit.","support":"~400 words reflect on where the classical picture breaks.","llc":"Present the derivation and defend the limit live."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAE12'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAE13 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Compute the Fourier transform of a chosen signal by hand and note WHY it loses time-localisation.","deliverable_notes":"The transform + one line on the time-localisation the Fourier view loses, for the named signal."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Apply a wavelet transform (Haar) to a REAL short signal you sample and show it captures both time and frequency.","deliverable_notes":"Your real sampled signal + its Haar coefficients + one line on the time-frequency read."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Construct cardinal B-splines of chosen order by hand and verify the refinement (two-scale) relation.","deliverable_notes":"The B-splines + the refinement-relation check, on paper."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Decompose a real signal into approximation + detail with a wavelet and reconstruct it, measuring the error.","deliverable_notes":"The decomposition/reconstruction + the reconstruction error on your real signal."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Use wavelet thresholding to denoise or compress a real signal you captured; quantify what you kept/discarded.","deliverable_notes":"The denoised/compressed signal + the compression ratio / SNR gain on your real data."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Wavelets on a Signal I Captured","primary":"Record a REAL signal yourself (audio, ECG-like pulse, a price/temperature series, an image row), apply a wavelet transform, and USE it for a real task — denoising, compression, or detecting a transient — quantifying the benefit.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the wavelet saw that a plain Fourier transform could not.","llc":"Play/show the real signal before and after; faculty pick a threshold and you re-run and defend the trade-off live."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Time-Frequency, Made Visible","primary":"Take one real non-stationary signal (a chirp, speech, a changing vibration) and produce its time-frequency picture with wavelets, showing where and when each frequency lives.","support":"~400 words reflect on the uncertainty trade-off you navigated.","llc":"Present the scalogram and answer what changes at a different scale."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Compression I Can Defend","primary":"Compress a real signal/image with wavelet thresholding to a target ratio and defend the visual/audible quality kept.","support":"~400 words reflect on which coefficients mattered and why.","llc":"Live-compress at a faculty-chosen ratio and justify the result."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"B-Splines and the Refinement Relation","primary":"Construct cardinal B-spline wavelets by hand, verify the two-scale relation, and use them on a real interpolation task.","support":"~400 words reflect on why splines give smooth multiresolution.","llc":"Present the construction and defend the refinement step live."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Denoising, Measured","primary":"Add known noise to a real clean signal, denoise with wavelets, and measure the SNR improvement objectively.","support":"~400 words reflect on the bias–variance of the threshold choice.","llc":"Demo the denoising; faculty change the noise level and you re-tune."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26PMAE13'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26PMAE14 · m-sc-mathematics ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Build an Excel model of a REAL small system you know (a shop's monthly cash flow, a hostel mess's consumption, your own budget) from real numbers.","deliverable_notes":"The Excel model + the real numbers behind it. Your name in a cell."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Add structure to your model (formulas, lookups, scenarios) so changing one real input propagates correctly.","deliverable_notes":"The working model with a driver input + a screenshot showing propagation."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Add a Monte-Carlo / random element to simulate uncertainty in your real model and run many trials.","deliverable_notes":"The simulation + the distribution of outcomes over trials for your real system."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Model a real financial or operations decision (a loan, an inventory reorder, a queue) and read the model's recommendation.","deliverable_notes":"The decision model + its recommendation on your real case."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Use Solver/optimization and scenario analysis to find the best decision and its sensitivity in your model.","deliverable_notes":"The optimised decision + a scenario/sensitivity table."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Model That Advised a Real Decision","primary":"Build an Excel model of a REAL decision someone you know actually faces (a small business's pricing/inventory, a family budget/loan, an event's logistics), simulate the uncertainty, optimise the choice, and DELIVER the recommendation to that real person.","support":"~400 words reflect on whether they found it useful and where the model over-simplified.","llc":"Live-drive the model; faculty change a real input (a cost rises, demand drops) and you show the new recommendation and its sensitivity."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Uncertainty, Simulated","primary":"Take a real quantity with genuine uncertainty, build a Monte-Carlo model, and report the outcome distribution and the chance of a bad case.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the simulation revealed that a point estimate hid.","llc":"Run more trials live and show the estimate stabilising."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"Optimisation Inside a Real Model","primary":"Use Solver on a real operations/finance model to find the optimal decision and certify it against the constraints.","support":"~400 words reflect on which constraint was binding.","llc":"Present; faculty relax a constraint and you re-optimise live."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Scenario Analysis for a Real Plan","primary":"Build best/expected/worst scenarios for a real plan and report the range and the triggers that flip the decision.","support":"~400 words reflect on what the plan is most fragile to.","llc":"Present the scenarios; faculty pick a shock and you show the outcome."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Model Validated Against Reality","primary":"Build a model whose past predictions you can check against what actually happened, and quantify the model's error.","support":"~400 words reflect on why it erred and how you'd improve it.","llc":"Present the back-test and defend the error honestly."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
