  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Business on My Street","primary":"Choose ONE real business you can visit repeatedly. Across the semester, gather real evidence — photos, the owner's own words, a hand-sketched layout, their actual challenge.","support":"~400 words on its form, location logic, ethics, and one improvement the course suggests.","llc":"Show the cohort the real business through your evidence and the owner's voice; answer questions."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Why This Industry Is Here","primary":"Pick ONE named industry cluster (Erode textiles, Salem steel, Namakkal poultry). Gather real evidence — a hand-drawn map, photos, one named business inside it, one worker's words.","support":"~400 words on the location factors and what would happen if one changed.","llc":"Present your map and evidence; defend your factor analysis."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"A Company I Chose to Follow","primary":"Track ONE listed company whose product is in your daily life — your own dated price table across the semester, real news events, your own annotations.","support":"~400 words on how the market values it and one event that moved it.","llc":"Show your tracking chart and walk the cohort through what you learned about markets and your own consumption."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Association That Protects Its Own","primary":"Find ONE real trade association / cooperative connected to your family or town. Interview a named member (consent + phone-verifiable), get their words on what collective action gives them.","support":"~400 words on how it embodies the Unit V ideas.","llc":"Play or read the member's voice; tell the cohort what one person cannot do alone."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Two Ways to Run the Same Trade","primary":"Find TWO real businesses in the same trade organised differently (a sole-trader tea stall vs. a franchised cafe). Gather evidence from both — photos, the two owners' words, what each can and cannot do.","support":"~400 words contrasting their forms.","llc":"Present both, side by side, and judge which form fits which owner's goal under questions."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCCNM1'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

