    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Collect THREE real advertisements you actually saw this week — a Tamil TV ad, a hoarding in your town, a shop's poster, a phone pop-up — and identify each one's medium and objective.","deliverable_notes":"The three real ads (photos) + one line each naming medium and objective. The collected ads are the deliverable, not an essay."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Talk to ONE real business owner near you about who makes their advertising — a named shop, salon, or workshop — do they use an agency, a relative, or make it themselves?","deliverable_notes":"The named business + one line on how they advertise and why. A photo of their advertising strengthens it."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Find ONE real advertisement you think is misleading or plays on Indian values/culture, and ONE you think is honest and positive. Both from your real surroundings.","deliverable_notes":"The two real ads (photos) + one line each on why one troubles you ethically and one doesn't. Your own judgment, briefly stated."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Pick ONE brand you personally use and trace how its advertising builds its image — collect its ads and map them to the AIDA steps (Attention, Interest, Desire, Action).","deliverable_notes":"The named brand + its ads + a hand-drawn AIDA map. Two sentences on which step its advertising does best."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Write ONE real advertisement copy + rough layout for a real local business you know (a shop, a tuition centre, a food cart) — as if they hired you.","deliverable_notes":"Your own hand-made ad copy + layout sketch for the named real business. Show it to the owner if you can and note their reaction. The made ad is the deliverable."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Advertising of One Real Business","primary":"Choose ONE real local business you can visit. Collect all its real advertising (photos), interview the owner (consent + contact) about what works, and design ONE improved ad yourself by hand. Bring the collected ads + owner's words + your ad.","support":"~400 words on its advertising strategy and your improvement.","llc":"Show the real ads and your redesign; defend your choices."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"A Brand I Followed and Decoded","primary":"Pick ONE brand in your daily life. Collect its real ads across the semester, map them to AIDA and brand-building yourself, and track one real campaign. Bring your collection and hand-drawn analysis.","support":"~400 words on how it builds brand image.","llc":"Present your ad collection and walk the cohort through the strategy you decoded."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Ad That Troubled Me","primary":"Find ONE real advertisement in your surroundings that raises an ethical/cultural issue. Interview two real people (consent + contact) about how they read it. Bring the ad + their differing words.","support":"~400 words on the social aspects of advertising.","llc":"Show the ad, play the two views, lead a short cohort discussion. AI cannot interview YOUR neighbours."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"I Made a Real Ad for a Real Shop","primary":"Offer to design real advertising for ONE named local business (shop, cart, tuition centre) that needs it. Make the copy + layout by hand, show the owner, capture their reaction. Bring your ad + their response.","support":"~400 words on the copywriting and layout principles you used.","llc":"Present your ad and the owner's real reaction."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Two Ads, Two Strategies","primary":"Find TWO real ads for competing products/shops in your area organised differently. Collect both, interview which one actually pulled a real customer you can name. Bring both ads + the customer's words.","support":"~400 words contrasting their strategies.","llc":"Present both and judge which worked, under questions."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCCNM2'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCCS01 · b-com-computer-application ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Walk through ONE local industry you can name (a handloom beside a power loom in Komarapalayam, a hand-written kirana beside a billing-software shop) and find examples of Industry 1.0 to 4.0 sitting side by side.","deliverable_notes":"Photos of two setups — one older/manual and one newer/automated + a hand-drawn timeline placing each on the 1.0-to-4.0 line. Write 3-4 sentences naming the one Industry 4.0 technology (automation, sensors, software) you actually saw at work."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Install ONE AI app from the course list (the OCR Image-to-Text-to-Speech app, or Grammarly) and run it on a REAL document from your life — a fee receipt, a Tamil notice, a handwritten note.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the app's output on YOUR document + the document named. Write 3-4 sentences on what the AI got right, what it got wrong (especially with Tamil script), and one daily use you would actually keep."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Pick ONE service you use that quietly collects big data — your phone's Maps timeline, a UPI app's history, a shop's loyalty SMS. Open it and look at how much it holds on you.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot (personal details masked) showing the scale of the data. Write 3-4 sentences mapping what you see to the big-data characteristics (volume, velocity, variety) and one way that business uses your data to decide something."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Find ONE real IoT use near you that affects a person's wellbeing — a farmer's soil/drip sensor, a smartwatch tracking a relative's heart rate, a cold-storage temperature alert. Ask the user how it helps.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of the device + the user's first name and what they told you. Write 3-4 sentences on which IoT application area it is (agriculture, healthcare, logistics) and how it changed a real decision that person makes."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Interview ONE named worker whose job is changing because of automation (a bank teller, a mill worker, a shopkeeper who moved online). Ask what new skill they had to learn to keep up.","deliverable_notes":"Their words quoted + their first name and contact (with consent). Write 3-4 sentences on the skill they picked up and the ONE new skill you will learn to stay employable in an Industry 4.0 world."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"Two Looms, Two Centuries","primary":"Document ONE real side-by-side of a manual and an automated version of the same local trade — photograph both, interview a worker at each (named, consented). Bring your photos and notes.","support":"~400 words placing each on the Industry 1.0-to-4.0 line and what changed for the worker.","llc":"Show the two setups and tell the cohort which technology made the leap. AI cannot photograph YOUR town's real looms."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"I Made AI Read My Life","primary":"Run two AI apps (OCR, Grammarly) on a set of YOUR own real documents — including Tamil ones — and log exactly where each app succeeds and fails. Bring the screenshots.","support":"~400 words on AI's strengths and its blind spots with local scripts and content.","llc":"Demo one app live on a document the faculty hands you and report what it does."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Data Trail I Leave","primary":"Audit the big data ONE real app holds on you personally — screenshot the scale (details masked) and describe how it is collected and used.","support":"~400 words mapping it to volume/velocity/variety and the privacy trade-off you accept.","llc":"Present your own data trail and one setting you changed after seeing it."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Sensor That Watches My Field","primary":"Find ONE real IoT deployment in agriculture, healthcare, or storage near you and interview the named user about how it changed their decisions. Bring a photo of the device and consented notes.","support":"~400 words on the IoT application area and its real benefit and cost.","llc":"Present the device and the user's story to the cohort."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Job That Had to Change","primary":"Interview ONE named worker whose role Industry 4.0 reshaped — record consented voice and a phone-verifiable contact. Reconstruct what their job was, what automation changed, and what they learned.","support":"~400 words on the human impact of Industry 4.0 on that worker.","llc":"Play their words and present the one skill you are learning because of what they told you."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCCS01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCCS02 · b-com-computer-application ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Visit ONE workplace you can name (a mill, a workshop, a factory canteen, even the college kitchen) and check it against Factories Act basics — drinking water, ventilation, first-aid, restrooms, and working hours.","deliverable_notes":"A hand-written checklist of what is present versus missing + the workplace name. Write 3-4 sentences on one welfare provision that was well met and one that was not, and whether women or young workers are employed within the legal hours."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Find ONE named person who has lived through an industrial dispute — a strike, layoff, retrenchment, or lockout (a mill worker, a union member, a supervisor). Ask what happened and how it ended.","deliverable_notes":"Their words quoted + their first name and contact (with consent). Write 3-4 sentences classifying the event (strike, lockout, layoff, retrenchment, closure) and how it was settled — through an award, a settlement, or otherwise."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Talk to ONE named worker or family who faced a workplace injury or accident. Ask whether compensation was paid and how the amount was worked out.","deliverable_notes":"Your written account + the person's first name and contact (with consent). Write 3-4 sentences on the type of disablement (permanent, partial, temporary) and whether the employer's liability under the Workmen's Compensation Act was actually met."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Get ONE real salary payslip (yours, a family member's, or a willing worker's) that shows ESI and EPF deductions, with personal details masked.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of the payslip (name and number masked) + a hand-written breakdown of the ESI and EPF amounts and who contributes — employee versus employer. Write 3-4 sentences on what benefit each deduction actually buys the worker."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Ask ONE named employee of a factory or shop whether they get an annual bonus, roughly how much, and when (the Deepavali bonus is common here).","deliverable_notes":"Your notes + the person's first name and workplace. Write 3-4 sentences relating their bonus to the Payment of Bonus Act — the minimum 8.33%, who is eligible, the salary ceiling — and whether what they get matches the law."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Factory Floor, Section by Section","primary":"Audit ONE real named workplace against the Factories Act — health, safety, welfare, hours — with a signed checklist and photos (with permission).","support":"~400 words on where the workplace complies and where it falls short for women and young workers.","llc":"Present your section-by-section findings and one change you would demand. AI cannot inspect YOUR local factory's real conditions."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Strike I Only Read About, Then Met","primary":"Interview a named worker who lived through a real strike, layoff, or retrenchment — record consented voice and a phone-verifiable contact. Reconstruct the dispute and its settlement.","support":"~400 words classifying it under the Industrial Disputes Act.","llc":"Play their words and present how the Act's machinery did or did not protect them."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Accident and the Compensation","primary":"Document ONE real workplace-injury case by interviewing the worker or family (named, consented, contactable). Reconstruct the disablement type and the compensation claimed and paid.","support":"~400 words applying the Workmen's Compensation Act to the facts.","llc":"Present the case and defend whether the settlement was fair."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"What My Payslip Really Says","primary":"Decode ONE real payslip (details masked) — break down every ESI and EPF figure, who contributes, and what each buys. Bring the masked payslip.","support":"~400 words connecting the deductions to the ESI Act 1948 and EPF Act 1952 benefits.","llc":"Show the payslip and explain to the cohort what their future first salary slip will hide."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Deepavali Bonus, By The Act","primary":"Find ONE real worker who receives an annual bonus and reconstruct how it is computed against the Payment of Bonus Act (named, consented). Bring their described figures.","support":"~400 words on eligibility, the 8.33% floor, and the salary ceiling for their case.","llc":"Present whether their real bonus matches the law and where it deviates."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCCS02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCSC01 · b-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Walk to ONE real shop or the bus board near you (name it — e.g. the tea stall at Kavandampatti bus stand) and copy down the actual price of 5 items OR the real timings of 5 buses. Write a small Python program using variables, input(), operators and type conversion that computes something real from your numbers (total bill, change from Rs.100, average price).","deliverable_notes":"A terminal screenshot of YOUR program running on the real numbers you copied, plus a phone photo of the shop board / bus board you took them from (your hand in frame). Write 3-4 sentences: which built-in data types you used, where input() forced a type conversion, and one operator you used and why."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Pick a REAL rule from your daily life that changes with conditions — bus fare by age (child/adult/senior concession), an electricity slab, or the mess veg/non-veg day. Ask a named person for the exact rule. Write a program with if-elif-else that reproduces it, and loop it over 4-5 real cases.","deliverable_notes":"Running-code screenshot showing several real inputs each landing on a different branch, plus the rule source (a photo of the fare chart, or the quoted sentence from the named person who told you). 3-4 sentences: which branches you wrote, and one input that fell to the else path."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Build your OWN module — a .py file with 2-3 functions that do a real chore for your family or hostel (format a Tamil+English name, split a full name, count vowels, or make a ready-to-send message). Import it into a second program, call your functions, and run dir() on the module.","deliverable_notes":"Screenshots of both files and the terminal showing your module imported plus the dir() output listing YOUR function names. 3-4 sentences: what your module does, why you split the work into functions, and one name dir() showed that you did not define."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Collect a real dataset from your own class: ask 8 real classmates their first name plus one true attribute (roll number, home town, or favourite subject). Store it as a list of dictionaries. Write code that answers a real question — how many are from Salem, or whose name is longest.","deliverable_notes":"The running program plus a photo of the handwritten list you actually collected (classmates' names visible). 3-4 sentences: why a dictionary fit each record better than a plain list, one lookup you performed, and one thing that was awkward to store."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Find ONE real person near you who keeps a paper record — a grandmother's medicine schedule, a shopkeeper's daily-sales notebook, a mess-in-charge's headcount register. With their permission, write a Python program that writes their real entries to a file and reads them back.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of the file your program created (with their real data), a photo of the original paper record, and the named person. 3-4 sentences: what you wrote and read back, why a file beats retyping every day, and one thing they told you about keeping records."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Program My Street Needed","primary":"Build a working Python tool for ONE named real user near you — a bill calculator for a named tea stall, a fine calculator, a name-sorter for a temple committee. Record a short clip of the real person trying it and quote their reaction.","support":"~400 words tying your code to Units I-V and what changed after real use.","llc":"Demo the running tool to the cohort and read your user's words. AI can write the code; it cannot make your named user try it."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Every Rupee, Counted","primary":"Keep a real handwritten log of your own spending for 7 days, then build a Python program (dictionaries + a file) that stores and analyses it — daily total, biggest category, cheapest day. Bring the paper log and the running program.","support":"~400 words on what the numbers revealed and which Python concepts carried the work.","llc":"Show your 7-day log and let the cohort question your totals live. AI cannot spend your week for you."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Voice in the Code","primary":"Interview ONE named elder about how they calculated before computers — shop arithmetic, land measurement, ration weighing. Record their voice with consent, get a phone-verifiable contact, quote them in their language. Then write a Python program that does the same calculation.","support":"~400 words linking their method to your code.","llc":"Play or read their words and show your program running. AI can describe the genre; it cannot interview YOUR elder."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"My Module, My Reuse","primary":"Find a chore your hostel or family repeats, build a reusable module of 2-3 functions that solves it, and demo it being imported into TWO different programs you wrote. Show dir() proving your functions.","support":"~400 words on why you split the code this way and what reuse saved.","llc":"Import your module live into a fresh program and run it in front of the cohort. AI can generate a module; it cannot prove it saved YOUR repeated chore."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"From Paper to Python","primary":"Take a real paper register from a named source — an attendance sheet, a sales book, a cricket scorecard. Digitise it with file handling and dictionaries so it can be searched and updated. Bring the original paper and the file your code produced.","support":"~400 words on what became easier and what was lost in translation.","llc":"Show before (paper) and after (program) and answer questions on your file format. AI cannot read YOUR named register."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCSC01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCSC02 · b-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities, one per Unit, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"I","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Find a real linear chain around you — the shops in a row on your named street, the compartments of a train you saw, or the steps of making morning tea. Model it as a linked list: draw each node (its data + an arrow to the next) BY HAND. Then show one insertion and one deletion on your drawing.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-drawn linked list of your real chain (name the street or shops), plus a second drawing showing an insert and a delete. 3-4 sentences: what each node holds, what the 'next' pointer points to, and one reason a linked list beats a fixed array for this chain."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"II","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Go to a REAL queue (a named ration shop, bus stand, or temple line) and a REAL stack (plates, vessels, notebooks). Watch the true order things enter and leave. Record it.","deliverable_notes":"A photo of the real queue and the real stack, plus a hand-written trace of enqueue/dequeue and push/pop in the exact order you observed. 3-4 sentences: which one is FIFO and which is LIFO, and one real moment where someone was let ahead (a priority queue breaking plain FIFO)."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"III","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Draw your REAL family tree across 3 generations with named relatives, OR your college hierarchy (Principal to HoD to staff, named). Then take 7 real numbers — relatives' ages or classmates' roll numbers — and build a Binary Search Tree BY HAND, inserting in the order you collected them.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-drawn family/organisation tree with real names, plus the hand-built BST of your 7 real numbers. 3-4 sentences: name the root and one leaf, and explain how a different insertion order would have changed the BST's shape."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"IV","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Map a REAL network you belong to — your walk from home to college (junctions are vertices, roads are edges) OR your real friend group (people are vertices, 'talks to' is an edge). Name every node. Then run a BFS from one node BY HAND and write the visit order.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-drawn graph with real place or people names, plus your BFS visit order. 3-4 sentences: is it directed?, name one cycle you found, and say what the BFS order revealed about who or what is 'closest' to your starting node."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"V","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Take a real deck of playing cards OR 10-15 of your classmates' actual roll-number slips. Physically sort them TWO ways (for example selection sort, then insertion sort) on the SAME shuffle, counting the comparisons your hands make each time.","deliverable_notes":"A photo taken mid-sort, plus a hand tally of comparisons for each method on the identical shuffle. 3-4 sentences: which method took fewer steps on your data, and one case (an already-sorted pile) where the slower method would actually win."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Structure Under My Street","primary":"Pick ONE real system near you — the shop row on a named street, local bus routes, a water pipeline, your family tree — and model it fully with the data structure that fits best (list, tree, or graph), drawn from your own observation.","support":"~400 words defending why that structure beat the alternatives.","llc":"Present your model and defend the structure choice under the cohort's questions. AI can name structures; it cannot map YOUR street."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Sorted by Hand, Then by Code","primary":"Collect a real dataset you gathered yourself (classmates' marks or heights, 12-15 values). Sort it TWO ways by hand, counting comparisons, then trace the same two algorithms on paper. Bring the raw collected data.","support":"~400 words comparing the step counts and Big-O behaviour you observed.","llc":"Show your tallies and defend which algorithm you'd choose for your data. AI cannot gather YOUR classmates' numbers."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Queue That Runs My Town","primary":"Study a real queue at a NAMED place — a ration shop, hospital OP, or bank. Interview the person managing it (phone-verifiable), record how they decide who goes next, and model it as a queue or priority queue.","support":"~400 words on where reality matched the ADT and where it did not.","llc":"Play or quote the manager and show your model. AI cannot interview YOUR queue manager."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"My Family Tree as a BST","primary":"Build your real 3-generation family tree with named relatives, then re-encode their ages as a Binary Search Tree built by hand. Show pre-order, in-order and post-order traversals.","support":"~400 words on what in-order traversal revealed and how insertion order shaped the tree.","llc":"Present both trees and answer traversal questions live. AI cannot know YOUR relatives' ages."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Map I Walk","primary":"Turn your real daily route (home to college, 6-8 named junctions) into a graph with an adjacency list you built from walking it. Run BFS and DFS by hand and find the fewest-hop path.","support":"~400 words on how BFS and DFS orders differed and what 'shortest hops' meant on the ground.","llc":"Show your map and trace a traversal on request. AI cannot walk YOUR route."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCSC02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCSCP01 · b-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Write and RUN a Python program that reads YOUR real details via input() — your name, your actual height in cm and weight in kg, or a real bill from your pocket — and computes something with operators (BMI, change, or a unit conversion). Print your own name in the output line.","deliverable_notes":"A terminal screenshot showing the program running on YOUR real numbers with your name printed, plus the source code. 3-4 sentences: one operator you used, where a type conversion from input() was needed, and one bug you hit and how you fixed it."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-5","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Collect a real list of numbers — your last 5 test marks, or 5 days of real temperature from your phone/a thermometer — and write a program that classifies each with if-elif-else inside a loop, using break or continue on a real condition (stop at the first fail).","deliverable_notes":"A running screenshot with your real numbers, plus the source. 3-4 sentences: which branch each value took, exactly where break or continue fired and why, and why a loop beat writing the check five separate times."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 6-7","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Write a recursive function you can hand-check on real input — factorial of the number of steps in your morning routine, the sum of your family members' ages, or reversing your own name. Hand-trace the recursion on paper BEFORE you run the code.","deliverable_notes":"The hand-traced recursion (paper, showing each call and return) plus the terminal output of the function running on YOUR real input. 3-4 sentences: name the base case, describe one recursive call, and say whether your paper trace matched what the code printed."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 8-10","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Build one program that stores a short real dataset in an array, runs a string operation on real text (count the words in a real WhatsApp message you wrote, or clean a real list of names), and calls one function pulled from your OWN module.","deliverable_notes":"A running screenshot with the real text visible, plus your module file. 3-4 sentences: one array operation you used, one built-in string method, and why you moved a function out into a module instead of leaving it inline."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 11-14","finks_dimension":"Caring","task":"Build a small real record-keeper for someone who needs it — a mess menu tracker, a grandmother's medicine list, or a self-help-group contribution register with real names and amounts. Use a dictionary/list, write the records to a file, and read them back.","deliverable_notes":"A terminal screenshot, the file your code wrote (real data inside), and a photo of the paper source or the named real person. 3-4 sentences: why you chose a dict over a list, what file persistence gave the person, and one feature you would add if they asked."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"The Tool I Built for a Real Person","primary":"Build a complete Python program for ONE named real user near you and demo it live to them — a bill helper for a named shop, a marks tracker for a classmate, a coupon counter for the mess. Capture their reaction.","support":"~400 words on the concepts (loops, functions, files) you used and what real use changed.","llc":"Run the tool in front of the cohort and quote your user. AI cannot make YOUR named user try it."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"Seven Days, One Program","primary":"Keep 7 days of your own real data by hand (spending, steps, or study hours), then build a program that stores it in a file and analyses it with dictionaries — totals, averages, best day. Bring the handwritten log.","support":"~400 words on what the analysis showed and which lab skills carried it.","llc":"Show the log and the running program and take questions on your totals. AI cannot live YOUR week."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"My Recursion, Traced by Hand","primary":"Choose a genuinely recursive real task, hand-trace the full call stack on paper for YOUR real input, then build and run the function so the output matches your trace.","support":"~400 words on the base case, the recursive step, and where a wrong base case would loop forever.","llc":"Walk the cohort through your paper stack and run the code alongside it. AI can write recursion; it cannot prove YOUR hand-trace matched."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"From Notebook to Program","primary":"Take a real paper register from a named source — attendance, sales, or a scorecard — and digitise it with files and dictionaries so it can be searched and updated. Bring the original notebook.","support":"~400 words on what became easier and what structure you chose for the file.","llc":"Show paper-then-program and answer questions on your data format. AI cannot read YOUR named register."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"The Module Library of My Making","primary":"Build ONE module holding 3 reusable functions that each solve a real repeated chore (name formatting, a small calculation, a message builder), then demo importing it into two different programs you wrote.","support":"~400 words on why you grouped them and what dir() showed.","llc":"Import your module live into a fresh program and run all three functions. AI can generate functions; it cannot prove they solved YOUR chores."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCSCP01'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCSCP02 · b-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Lab 1-2","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Implement a linked-list-based Stack OR Queue and feed it a REAL sequence you observed — the order of 6 buses leaving Kavandampatti stand, or plates stacked at your mess. Push/enqueue them in the true order, then pop/dequeue, printing each step.","deliverable_notes":"A terminal screenshot showing your real sequence going in and coming out, the source, and a one-line note naming where the real sequence came from. 3-4 sentences: FIFO vs LIFO as it played out in your run, one edge case (popping an empty structure), and what came out first."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Lab 3-4","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Take a REAL arithmetic expression from your life — a shop bill formula such as 3*20+2*15 — and run your infix-to-postfix converter and evaluator on it (the answer must match the real bill). Separately, model a real 'someone goes first' case (an elder let ahead) with your priority queue.","deliverable_notes":"A terminal screenshot showing YOUR real expression converted and evaluated to the correct total, plus the priority-queue run. 3-4 sentences: one operator-precedence step from the conversion, and who your priority queue served first and why."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Lab 5-6","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Collect 7-10 REAL numbers (classmates' roll numbers or relatives' ages, in the order you asked them). Insert them into your BST program; then insert the SAME numbers into your AVL program and watch it rotate to stay balanced.","deliverable_notes":"Terminal output of both trees (or their traversals) on the identical real numbers, plus a hand-drawn before/after-rotation sketch. 3-4 sentences: where the AVL rotated, why the plain BST became unbalanced on your order, and what the in-order traversal gave you."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Lab 7","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Build the adjacency list of a REAL small network — your named walk from home to college (5-8 junctions) or your real friend group ('talks to' edges). Run your BFS and DFS programs from one named node.","deliverable_notes":"Terminal output showing the BFS and DFS visit orders, plus your hand-drawn graph with real names. 3-4 sentences: how the two visit orders differed, one node that turned out 'far' in BFS, and what this network actually is in your life."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Lab 8-9","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Take a real unsorted list you collected (classmates' marks or heights) and run bubble, selection and insertion sort with a comparison counter added to each, on the SAME input. Then binary-search for one real value in the sorted result.","deliverable_notes":"A terminal screenshot showing the comparison count for each sort on your real data, and the binary-search hit or miss. 3-4 sentences: which sort made the fewest comparisons on your data, why binary search needed the sort first, and one value that was not found."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"One Real System, the Right Structure","primary":"Model a real named system near you with the ADT that fits best — a queue at a named shop, a family tree, your route graph — then build and RUN the code on data you collected yourself.","support":"~400 words defending the structure against the alternatives you rejected.","llc":"Run the program and defend the choice under the cohort's questions. AI can code an ADT; it cannot choose it for YOUR named system."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"The Same Numbers, Two Trees","primary":"Collect 10-12 real numbers (roll numbers or ages) and run them through both your BST and your AVL program in the same insertion order. Bring the raw collected numbers and screenshots of both trees.","support":"~400 words on where and why the AVL rotated and what balancing bought you.","llc":"Show both trees and trace a rotation live. AI cannot gather YOUR real numbers."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"My Town's Queue, in Code","primary":"Study a real queue at a NAMED place, interview the person who runs it (phone-verifiable) about who they let go first, then build a priority-queue simulation that reproduces their rule and RUN it.","support":"~400 words on where your ADT matched reality and where it broke.","llc":"Quote the manager and run your simulation. AI cannot interview YOUR queue manager."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"The Map I Walk, Searched","primary":"Encode your real daily route as a graph (named junctions) from walking it, run BFS and DFS in code, and search for the fewest-hop path between two points. Verify the result against the real walk.","support":"~400 words comparing your code's path to the route you actually take.","llc":"Show the map and run a traversal on request. AI cannot walk YOUR route."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"Which Sort Wins on My Data","primary":"Collect a real dataset you gathered, instrument bubble, selection and insertion sort with counters, RUN them all on the identical input, and record real comparison counts.","support":"~400 words defending which sort you would ship for data like yours, referencing Big-O and your measured counts.","llc":"Show the counts and defend your choice under questioning. AI cannot collect YOUR dataset or run YOUR machine."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCSCP02'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

-- ── 26UCSGEP04 · b-sc-computer-science ──
update public.bos_course_syllabi
set
  concept_applications = $j${
    "intro_note": "Five short Fink's-shaped activities anchored to the lab experiments, conducted as formative learning during the semester. Not separately graded as line items; faculty may credit toward the Activities row (5 marks) at the BoS Chairman's discretion. They build the practice that culminates in the Capstone. The deliverable for each is the evidence — a photo, a reading, a hand-drawn sketch, a data table, a quoted voice, running code — plus three or four sentences, not an essay. Every task requires a named local place, a named person, a real measurement, or a hand-made artefact that an AI cannot fabricate.",
    "activities": [
      {"sno":1,"unit":"Programs 1-2","finks_dimension":"Foundational Knowledge","task":"Write and run programs 1 and 2 yourself — the Fahrenheit-Celsius converter and the nested-loop pyramid pattern. Feed the converter today's REAL Erode temperature from your phone, and set the pyramid height to the number of letters in your own name.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of YOUR terminal showing your name-length pyramid and today's real temperature converted + the two .py files with your own comments. Write 3-4 sentences on one bug you hit and how the loop or conditional logic fixed it."},
      {"sno":2,"unit":"Programs 3-4","finks_dimension":"Application","task":"Run programs 3 and 4 on data from your real life — enter YOUR own five subject marks into the grade program, and the real dimensions of a real object (your room, a book, a bangle) into the area program.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of both programs running on your real numbers + the object you measured named. Write 3-4 sentences on whether the grade output matched what you expected and one area you also worked out by hand to check the code was right."},
      {"sno":3,"unit":"Programs 5-6","finks_dimension":"Learning How to Learn","task":"Write and run the prime-numbers program and the recursive factorial. Then, by hand, trace how the recursion unfolds for factorial(5) BEFORE you run it.","deliverable_notes":"Your hand-written recursion trace (5 x 4 x 3 x ...) + a screenshot of the code's output matching it. Write 3-4 sentences on what recursion taught you that an ordinary loop did not, and how you would explain 'a function calling itself' to a classmate."},
      {"sno":4,"unit":"Programs 7-8","finks_dimension":"Integration","task":"Run program 7 (count even and odd numbers in an array of N) using the real roll numbers of your lab batch, and program 8 (a class that reverses a string word by word) on a real sentence you write.","deliverable_notes":"A screenshot of both outputs on YOUR data — your batch's roll numbers and your own sentence + the class code with your method names. Write 3-4 sentences on how the class (object) in program 8 differs from the plain function in program 7."},
      {"sno":5,"unit":"Programs 9-10","finks_dimension":"Human Dimension","task":"Run program 9 (copy the odd lines of a file into a new file) on a REAL text file you create — write 10 lines about your family. Then run program 10 and use Turtle graphics to draw your initials or a simple kolam.","deliverable_notes":"The two files (your original + the odd-lines output) + a screenshot of your Turtle drawing. Write 3-4 sentences on what the odd-line file kept and dropped, and one thing you changed in the Turtle code to make the drawing truly yours."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  capstone_project = $j${
    "intro_note": "Choose ONE of FIVE — Solo · 10 marks (Internal) · spans the semester · presented at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference. The Senior Learner introduces all five Capstones to the cohort in week 1. The assessment focuses on what AI cannot do. Each Capstone has three parts: (1) the AI-proof primary deliverable — a real measured object, a hand-drawn graph, a named-source interview with a phone-verifiable contact, running code, a real dataset, or a thing you built, as the option specifies; (2) a short ~400-word reflection — deliberately brief, enough to show your thinking, too short to be worth generating; and (3) a 5–7 minute presentation at the end-of-course Learners Led Conference (LLC) where you show the real thing and answer unscripted questions. A long polished essay is exactly what a language model produces best, so it is not the deliverable. No outside-community Public Exhibition — the LLC audience is the cohort and faculty.",
    "options": [
      {"option_no":1,"title":"A Program That Solves One Real Problem of Mine","primary":"Pick ONE real small problem in your own life — splitting a hostel mess bill, converting your marks to GPA, a countdown to your exam — and write a WORKING Python program that solves it using the lab constructs. Demo it live on real input.","support":"~400 words on your design choices and bugs.","llc":"Run it in front of the cohort and take one 'what if I enter this?' challenge from the faculty. AI can write code; it cannot pass YOUR live modification test."},
      {"option_no":2,"title":"My Code, Line by Line","primary":"Take ONE lab program, add your own comment explaining EVERY line, and prove you own it by modifying it live when the requirement changes. Bring the annotated file.","support":"~400 words explaining the logic in plain Tamil-English to a non-coder.","llc":"The faculty changes one requirement on the spot and you edit the code live to meet it."},
      {"option_no":3,"title":"The Bug Diary","primary":"Keep a real, dated log of every error you hit across the 10 programs — screenshot each actual error message and record how you fixed it. Bring the diary.","support":"~400 words on the three hardest bugs and the pattern behind them.","llc":"Show a real error screenshot and walk the cohort through your fix. AI-written code hides the errors YOU actually made."},
      {"option_no":4,"title":"Teaching Recursion With My Hands","primary":"Build a physical or hand-drawn model that explains recursion (using factorial) and defend it by running the code alongside. Bring the model.","support":"~400 words on how the code's call-stack matches your model.","llc":"Teach recursion to the cohort with your model, then run factorial live for a number they pick."},
      {"option_no":5,"title":"A Turtle Drawing Only I Could Make","primary":"Use Turtle graphics to draw something rooted in you — your name, your village's kolam pattern, your college logo — with code you wrote and can change. Bring the .py file.","support":"~400 words on how you translated a shape into loops and coordinates.","llc":"Generate the drawing live and modify one parameter (colour, size, angle) on request."}
    ]
  }$j$::jsonb,
  last_modified_at = now()
where course_code = '26UCSGEP04'
  and is_latest = true and is_archived = false;

